WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.669
- It's a free market economy

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.171
but it's also a
national security issue.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.215
- Okay well I tend to think--
- You're going to be an indepent

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:09.009
country, you need to be
independant of food.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.385
- I tend to think of
climate change

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:11.720
as a national security issue.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.929
- I do too.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.389
- They're not gonna
be able to do it

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:15.682
in time the way
they're doing it.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.892
And every time, well.
- Recognize and adapt

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:19.519
and change policies--
- Okay, let's...

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:22.355
Let's look at ourselves
right now, okay?

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:25.066
Let's say this road we're on

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.861
is the American
political system.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.947
And you see past
that bridge up there,

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:33.575
we have to get past that
bridge in the next minute,

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.412
except this is
what we're stuck on

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:39.247
because we can't get there.

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:41.207
And you know this
as well as I do.

00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:45.754
(rain pouring)

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:47.422
I know people in
their 20s and 30s

00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:49.924
that will not have kids,

00:00:49.924 --> 00:00:51.384
because they're like,

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.762
"I'm not gonna subject a
human being to what's coming."

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:57.098
Do you think my children
who are 15 years younger

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:58.767
than that are somehow gonna go,

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.436
"God I always wanted
my kids to grow up

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:04.272
in Mad Max eating
lizards off the road

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:07.317
and murdering their neighbors
to get a glass of water."

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.612
I mean, things are
gonna break down.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:14.407
- [Wesley] Wesley
the passion that you

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.076
and so many others
have on us this

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.286
is what gives me
hope in democracy

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:20.080
and hope in the
future of the country.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.791
That's what it's gonna
take to make change happen.

00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:25.251
(soft music)

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.464
- [Wesley Jr.] I think we're
living in a rigged system.

00:01:30.965 --> 00:01:32.509
And I think it's very hard

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:36.137
to break out of that rigged
system because money talks.

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:38.598
(soft music)

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:47.982
- I drove down from
San Francisco yesterday
on Highway Five

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:49.526
and it's bumper to bumper

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:52.320
for 300 miles on
(laughs) Highway Five.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.490
And you can think, God, that's
just a small part of it.

00:01:55.698 --> 00:01:58.868
So we know in these
big urban areas

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:03.289
like the Los Angeles Basin,
metropolitan New York,

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.126
we know that, we live
here for convenience.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.794
We live here because
civilization works,

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:10.839
but we also know it
is relatively fragile.

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:13.049
It depends on power,
it depends on water.

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:16.136
It depends on food, it
depends on warehousing.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.681
It depends on security and
people following the rules

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:21.182
and obeying the laws.

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:24.227
And it only takes small
disruptions to that.

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:27.397
Whether through natural
disaster, through climate change

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:30.692
through organized
crime or through war,

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.653
through exogenous forces.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.200
And you'd see catastrophe.

00:02:41.035 --> 00:02:44.038
- [Narrator] It's easy to
see how things can fall apart,

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:46.207
Long tunnels are now pitch dark.

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:48.251
(cars passing)

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.713
Communications and telephone
systems have collapsed.

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:53.506
On motorways cars
simply pull over,

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.216
if they find a
place with a signal.

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:57.677
(man speaking foreign language)

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.095
People now stand in line

00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:01.723
outside supermarkets,
not to go in,

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:04.017
they're just hoping that
it will eventually open.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.060
(people chattering)

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.021
(wind blows vehemently)

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:12.066
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.704
- Combination of the weight
of scientific evidence.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:28.041
And the dynamics of the
financial system suggest

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.918
that in the fullness of time,

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:33.713
climate change will threaten
financial resilience

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:36.299
and longer term prosperity.

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:39.719
- All the money in the
world will not save us

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.638
once it starts to fail.

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:42.931
And then people will discover

00:03:42.931 --> 00:03:45.558
that you can't eat
a flat screen TV

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.727
and you can't eat a
German luxury car.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.729
(pressing salt shaker)

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.064
(tries to open door)

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.649
- What are we gonna do?

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:56.653
(engine revving silently)

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:58.404
- It's war.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.616
And it's a much more serious
war than World War II

00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:04.827
because if we lose,
we're all dead.

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:06.120
I mean, you'll already be dead.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.081
So maybe it doesn't
concern you as much

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:10.792
but I don't want my
children to starve to death

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:13.461
- You're writing me
off really early.

00:04:13.461 --> 00:04:14.921
Please don't write me off
so early.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.798
- All right, 20 more years

00:04:17.006 --> 00:04:19.634
(children screaming)

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:20.802
- Looks good doesn't it?

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:21.803
- It does.

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:24.472
- It's a great part of America.

00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:25.974
It's like an American icon.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.979
My generation was
the Beach Boys.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:32.313
- Yeah.

00:04:32.313 --> 00:04:33.940
- Surfing USA.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.608
- Yeah.
(water splashes)

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:41.614
- So, you know, tourist heaven.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:42.490
- Yeah.

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:44.325
- But if I were a tourist
from another planet

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:47.203
and I was like, "Hey,
let's go visit earth."

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:48.871
And then my travel guy was like,

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:52.166
"well it's populated

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:57.171
by about 10 billion carnivorous
apes and they're armed."

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.133
- My goal is to see...
- Let's give you 20 more year

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:03.011
Is to see your children

00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.638
grown, educated, married

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.891
and I wanna have
great-grandchildren,
that's my goal.

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:11.352
- Okay, if you wanna
have great grandchildren,

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:13.980
what you have to do is
you have to help build

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.567
that environment that
they're gonna survive in.

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:21.863
- I want you to see the
facts as they appear to me.

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:23.823
I'm a national security guy.

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.908
That's what I've spent
my life working on

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:29.078
trying to protect
America, the constitution,

00:05:29.078 --> 00:05:32.248
the way we live and our futures

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:34.542
for our children
and grandchildren.

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:37.712
- I love my dad we're both
concerned about security.

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:39.839
We're both concerned
about the future

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.549
but there's definitely
a difference

00:05:41.549 --> 00:05:43.676
in the generational
perspective of this.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:48.181
Now I understand his generation
because they grew up,

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.516
right after World War
II, the baby boomers.

00:05:53.394 --> 00:05:56.064
They grew up in a world that
was constantly improving.

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:57.774
(car hooting)

00:05:57.774 --> 00:06:00.193
More rights, more money.

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.528
The economy constantly expanded.

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:10.787
They saw us go from fairly
primitive industrial society

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:13.915
to what we have today.

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:18.044
- There've been empires
that formed and collapsed.

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:24.759
And about 200 years ago,
mankind suddenly discovered

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:28.429
how to take energy more
effectively from the earth.

00:06:29.263 --> 00:06:32.058
In the start of
the industrial age.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:36.938
And it was coal
and it was England.

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:40.608
And it was a steam engine
that could use that coal

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:43.403
and it spread throughout
the whole world.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:45.279
We have taken that energy

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:47.323
and built this
civilization from it

00:06:48.616 --> 00:06:51.411
and the price that's being paid

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.288
is the carbonization
of the atmosphere

00:06:54.288 --> 00:06:55.665
and climate change.

00:07:00.461 --> 00:07:03.589
(car engine roaring)

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:09.846
- Yeah, that's the finale.

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:14.308
(upbeat music)

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:19.939
- You know, when you
say in World War II,

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:21.816
everybody chipped in
and did their part.

00:07:21.816 --> 00:07:23.943
And that was
seventy-five years ago.

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:29.615
Now we've been involved
in so far, a 19 year war.

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:32.452
And in that 19 year war,

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.787
we were told to go shopping

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.497
and to buy more stuff.

00:07:36.497 --> 00:07:38.332
We were never told
to save anything.

00:07:38.332 --> 00:07:40.668
We were never told to
contribute to a war effort.

00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:42.503
They even cut taxes

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.630
when we went to war
- That's right, twice.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.132
- Twice.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.636
(upbeat music)

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:56.142
With gambling, the house always
wins and it's a rigged game.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:57.643
It just is.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.312
Not unlike the banking system

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.522
in our political
system nowadays.

00:08:01.522 --> 00:08:02.857
It's all rigged. People don't

00:08:02.857 --> 00:08:04.817
have an actual
choice in a lot of stuff.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:06.903
Yeah, people have a
choice to go to Vegas.

00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:10.323
- When you ran for president,

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:13.826
I drove all over New
Mexico and other places.

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:16.370
All I could think
in 2003 was that,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:20.082
we can't actually
build an economy

00:08:20.082 --> 00:08:23.419
off stripping, gambling
and crystal meth.

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:25.838
And we've made a concerted
effort to do that

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:28.382
in this country for
the last 20 years.

00:08:28.382 --> 00:08:31.177
- You know, our business
caters to what people want.

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:37.892
American societies produced
enormous amounts of leisure.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.357
- They've tried to recreate
the feel of the Roman Empire

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:46.025
in the heart of Vegas.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:50.196
And interestingly it's
like late Roman Empire.

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:52.573
So it's right, as
everything collapsed.

00:08:57.745 --> 00:09:00.122
- We have the smarts
and the know-how

00:09:00.122 --> 00:09:01.916
and the conscience to know

00:09:01.916 --> 00:09:04.710
we have to sustain
and protect this earth

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.879
because this is where we live.

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:09.048
So we love civilization,

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:11.300
but we've got to change

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.971
the way we're approaching
our economic endeavors.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.265
- The world is suffering

00:09:17.265 --> 00:09:19.559
through the worst financial
crisis since 1930s.

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:21.602
- There's been a
lot of damage done.

00:09:21.602 --> 00:09:24.772
- They hear $700
billion package,

00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:26.566
and they immediately
think the next day

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:27.942
everything is gonna be better.

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:31.404
- You're witnessing
so much wealth loss.

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:33.114
- If you connect
the financial system

00:09:33.114 --> 00:09:36.075
to the other possible
shocks that are out there,

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:41.080
you realize it's,
we're walking on ice.

00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:42.957
- Panic coming
through the phones,

00:09:42.957 --> 00:09:45.293
on the floor, traders
just trying to rush

00:09:45.293 --> 00:09:47.253
to get to the point of sale,

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:48.588
to get rid of their stock.

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:50.965
- [Man] You know,
what people built up

00:09:50.965 --> 00:09:54.010
over years and years and
years, has been erased.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.637
(bell ringing)
(people applauding)

00:09:56.637 --> 00:10:01.809
- At some point, you run a
risk that something goes afoul.

00:10:02.476 --> 00:10:04.103
We're worried about Greek debt.

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:05.688
We're worried
about Italian debt.

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:07.189
- Spanish debt.

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:10.192
- I mean and so far, you
know, it's been finessed,

00:10:10.192 --> 00:10:13.029
but, and you know,

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:15.489
you can't see what the U.S
Treasury is doing behind.

00:10:15.489 --> 00:10:17.783
We're doing a lot with
the U.S Government

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:19.952
to keep the system working.

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:23.789
And you just don't know
what the limits are.

00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:25.291
- So if you think,

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:28.544
how can we lead the world
in fixing the economy

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:29.962
and fixing the climate?

00:10:29.962 --> 00:10:32.548
You have to make
the investments now.

00:10:32.548 --> 00:10:35.176
Because you can't wait
till you're like Somalia.

00:10:35.176 --> 00:10:36.719
I mean, you may notice

00:10:36.719 --> 00:10:39.138
Somalia doesn't have a lot
of infrastructure programs

00:10:39.138 --> 00:10:40.681
that they fund.

00:10:40.681 --> 00:10:43.643
They're not pushing the
boundaries in technology,

00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:46.062
and that's not because
people in Somalia are stupid,

00:10:46.062 --> 00:10:48.689
it's not just because they've
lived under civil war,

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:51.734
it's because they
don't have capital.

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:53.819
They don't have resources.

00:10:58.032 --> 00:11:01.827
- Capitalism is an
economic organization.

00:11:01.827 --> 00:11:04.246
It's a way of
organizing the economy

00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:09.251
that is focused on trying
to create future returns

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:11.879
over and above of the
investments I make today.

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:14.507
So it's all about expansion.

00:11:14.507 --> 00:11:16.008
It's all about making
more in the future

00:11:16.008 --> 00:11:17.426
than we currently have.

00:11:19.345 --> 00:11:21.013
- When you borrow money,

00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:22.682
well, what happens is,

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:25.017
the banks would like
to charge you interest

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:26.686
not only for the risk,

00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:30.231
but because they want
the capital to come
back with babies,

00:11:30.231 --> 00:11:33.234
not just to go and that's it.

00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:36.570
Because they want to make sure
that capital is increasing

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.698
and that's how you build wealth

00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:40.908
and that's how you
increase your assets.

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:46.080
- And it's that debt
that has enabled

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.457
all of this to be here.

00:11:48.457 --> 00:11:51.127
It's the accelerant
to economic growth.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:57.591
- Now, as long as the company

00:11:57.591 --> 00:11:59.593
can service that
debt, it's okay.

00:11:59.593 --> 00:12:00.720
But consumer debt,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:01.929
if it can't be
serviced, is a problem.

00:12:01.929 --> 00:12:03.139
Mortgage debt if it
can't be serviced,

00:12:03.139 --> 00:12:04.640
as we've seen is a problem,

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:05.975
and national debt, if
it can't be serviced,

00:12:05.975 --> 00:12:07.810
can be a national
security threat.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:11.605
- Alexa, how do we avert
a financial crisis?

00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:16.068
- [Alexa] Here's something
I found on Wikipedia.

00:12:16.068 --> 00:12:19.321
A financial crisis is any of
a broad variety of situations

00:12:19.321 --> 00:12:20.823
in which some financial assets

00:12:20.823 --> 00:12:23.284
suddenly lose a large part
of their nominal value.

00:12:25.453 --> 00:12:30.416
- Preparing for the future,
when the future is unknown,

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:33.127
is obviously difficult,

00:12:33.961 --> 00:12:38.299
but we are today taking steps

00:12:38.299 --> 00:12:42.261
that are clearly going
to make things worse.

00:12:42.261 --> 00:12:44.096
- Finance is really
making big bets

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:46.098
on an uncertain future.

00:12:46.098 --> 00:12:49.310
And you wanna be careful
about making too many bets,

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.145
especially when there
is some indication

00:12:51.145 --> 00:12:53.689
that the future might be very
different from the present.

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:58.152
(insects flying)

00:12:59.987 --> 00:13:02.615
(upbeat music)

00:13:03.657 --> 00:13:05.993
- [Wesley Jr.] So much
of our energy's caught up

00:13:05.993 --> 00:13:07.495
in the financial system

00:13:07.495 --> 00:13:11.332
that we've neglected the
investment in real things

00:13:11.332 --> 00:13:13.667
that are required to
take the country forward.

00:13:13.667 --> 00:13:17.379
- We do, we haven't really
come to grip with this yet,

00:13:17.379 --> 00:13:18.839
because the economic system,

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:21.050
the political system just
doesn't wanna face it.

00:13:24.845 --> 00:13:27.473
(upbeat music)

