00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.821
When people say, well,

00:17:52.821 --> 00:17:55.240
how do you know that the
climate models that we have now

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:57.993
will be accurate
in 30 years time?

00:17:57.993 --> 00:18:00.829
What I can tell you is that
the models that we were working

00:18:00.829 --> 00:18:04.958
on 30 years ago are actually
unbelievably accurate.

00:18:04.958 --> 00:18:07.419
(soft music)

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.005
What's happened
with the atmosphere,

00:18:10.005 --> 00:18:13.634
is we're seeing this type of
instability, kind of like,

00:18:13.634 --> 00:18:18.514
you know, if you had a
top just standing there,

00:18:18.514 --> 00:18:20.015
it'll just fall over.

00:18:20.015 --> 00:18:21.767
And so you're
seeing that type of,

00:18:21.767 --> 00:18:24.478
that type of wobbling
and instability.

00:18:24.478 --> 00:18:28.774
I don't exactly know how
unstable things are going to be,

00:18:28.774 --> 00:18:31.485
but to me, it's the
volatility and the instability

00:18:31.485 --> 00:18:33.612
that's even more
concerning than just

00:18:33.612 --> 00:18:37.950
the pure temperature
rise over time.

00:18:38.283 --> 00:18:42.162
Let's say that inflation
were to go over the top,

00:18:42.162 --> 00:18:43.956
stock market crashes.

00:18:43.956 --> 00:18:46.375
Let's say there are all kinds
