- Well,

00:34:06.002 --> 00:34:07.462
- The answer, say yes.

00:34:07.462 --> 00:34:08.504
- Yes.
- Okay, good.

00:34:08.504 --> 00:34:10.048
So here's a mortgage.

00:34:10.048 --> 00:34:12.967
Okay, so here's the money and--
- And by the way, this is just

00:34:12.967 --> 00:34:14.510
like a real bank,
because in the real bank,

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:16.179
they're not sure can
this guy pay for it?

00:34:16.179 --> 00:34:17.972
Just tell him yes, tell
him yes, come on,

00:34:17.972 --> 00:34:19.682
lets get the money.
- So it depends on paying.

00:34:19.682 --> 00:34:22.477
- We typically think that
bank takes deposits from us

00:34:22.477 --> 00:34:23.978
and then lends it out.

00:34:23.978 --> 00:34:26.814
The balance sheet equates
one entry on this side,

00:34:26.814 --> 00:34:29.317
another entry on that side, has
sort of claims to come back,

00:34:29.317 --> 00:34:32.070
and if it's really
comes back as expected,

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:33.529
they make a return on that.

00:34:33.529 --> 00:34:35.698
And it's basically making
money out of thin air.

00:34:36.365 --> 00:34:37.992
- In the old days,

00:34:37.992 --> 00:34:39.869
I would put this
mortgage, and I would say,

00:34:39.869 --> 00:34:41.037
"This is an asset for me,

00:34:41.037 --> 00:34:42.663
"because you're gonna
pay me every month

00:34:42.663 --> 00:34:44.248
"to pay this
mortgage off," right?

00:34:44.248 --> 00:34:45.875
- So you're telling all
your buddies you had that.

00:34:45.875 --> 00:34:47.502
- Well, I found a better
way to make money, okay?

00:34:47.502 --> 00:34:52.215
So the bank takes the IOU,
and the bank sells the IOU

00:34:53.174 --> 00:34:55.885
into the what's called
the secondary market.

00:34:56.219 --> 00:34:59.055
- Okay.
And it gets a fee for that.

00:34:59.055 --> 00:35:01.349
- Then once I have this
mortgage and maybe the mortgage

00:35:01.349 --> 00:35:03.351
for many other houses
in the neighborhood,

00:35:03.351 --> 00:35:05.686
and ideally from other
neighborhoods as well,

00:35:05.686 --> 00:35:08.731
I throw this into what we call
a special purpose vehicle,

00:35:08.731 --> 00:35:11.192
which really, in legal
terms, is typically a trust

00:35:11.192 --> 00:35:14.362
or a corporation, these
assets-shielding devices,

00:35:14.362 --> 00:35:16.239
and then you issue claims

00:35:16.239 --> 00:35:19.534
against these new legal
shells to investors.

00:35:19.534 --> 00:35:22.245
(retro jazz music)

00:35:22.245 --> 00:35:27.250
- They package these IOUs
up so there's many of them,

00:35:27.875 --> 00:35:31.712
and they sell them to people
like insurance companies

00:35:32.588 --> 00:35:37.593
or foreign banks, and
they can be leveraged.

00:35:37.593 --> 00:35:40.638
- Which means I can make
loans off these things,

00:35:40.638 --> 00:35:41.889
which are loans.

00:35:41.889 --> 00:35:43.933
- Yes, because you
have leverage here.

00:35:43.933 --> 00:35:45.977
So you can make more loans.

00:35:45.977 --> 00:35:48.771
So the system always
generates cash.

00:35:48.771 --> 00:35:51.607
(water splashing)

00:35:52.733 --> 00:35:54.277
They can reduce their risk

00:35:54.277 --> 00:35:56.612
because they're not
just buying a mortgage,

00:35:56.612 --> 00:35:58.239
they're buying a
package of mortgages.

00:35:58.239 --> 00:36:00.449
In some cases, they're
just buying a slice

00:36:00.449 --> 00:36:04.162
of the interest rate off
a package of mortgages.

00:36:04.162 --> 00:36:07.582
And so, all of these
are called derivatives.

00:36:07.582 --> 00:36:11.502
And everything works, as long
as there's an expectation

00:36:11.502 --> 00:36:13.421
that the loan will be repaid,

00:36:13.421 --> 00:36:17.800
that John will, at the
right time each month,

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:22.430
give the bank money to pay
down his share of the IOU.

00:36:22.430 --> 00:36:25.266
(old timey piano music)

00:36:25.266 --> 00:36:27.310
So the old way the
banking system worked,

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:30.104
it used to work that the banker
would look you in the eye

00:36:30.104 --> 00:36:31.647
and say, "Are you credit worthy?

00:36:31.647 --> 00:36:32.940
"Can I trust you?"

00:36:36.277 --> 00:36:38.446
- The banks really
aren't keeping

00:36:38.446 --> 00:36:40.114
the mortgages on
their books anymore.

00:36:40.114 --> 00:36:42.158
They're selling those mortgages

00:36:42.158 --> 00:36:43.367
so that they can be repackaged

00:36:43.367 --> 00:36:45.828
as other kinds of
financial instruments.

00:36:45.828 --> 00:36:48.539
So, they're able to
sort of pass the buck,

00:36:48.539 --> 00:36:50.625
pass the risk off
to the next person.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:55.046
- Depending on how they're
rated by the rating agencies,

00:36:55.046 --> 00:36:58.466
these are reputable
investment opportunities

00:36:58.466 --> 00:36:59.842
for retirement funds, sure.

00:36:59.842 --> 00:37:02.136
Yeah, pension funds,
sure, put 'em in here.

00:37:02.136 --> 00:37:03.971
- So the investors
are buying an interest

00:37:03.971 --> 00:37:06.307
in the cashflow that
comes from the homeowners

00:37:06.307 --> 00:37:08.559
into the pool that
we've created,

00:37:08.559 --> 00:37:10.686
but have sort of a claim
against this house as well

00:37:10.686 --> 00:37:12.480
because this cashflow is backed

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:14.357
by the mortgage
against the home.

00:37:14.357 --> 00:37:17.193
So something as
illiquid, as formidable

00:37:17.193 --> 00:37:18.819
as this building and
the land it stands on

00:37:18.819 --> 00:37:21.989
can become a globally
tradable financial asset

00:37:21.989 --> 00:37:23.658
through appropriate
legal coding.

00:37:26.577 --> 00:37:27.870
- This is your loan.

00:37:27.870 --> 00:37:30.331
It travels through this system.

00:37:31.249 --> 00:37:32.208
The bank has the loan,

00:37:32.208 --> 00:37:33.167
(stamp smashing)

00:37:33.167 --> 00:37:35.211
sells it, it becomes a security.

00:37:35.211 --> 00:37:37.088
It becomes part of
the balance sheet

00:37:37.088 --> 00:37:38.547
of money market mutual funds.

00:37:38.547 --> 00:37:42.093
These money market mutual
funds then basically exchange

00:37:42.093 --> 00:37:44.845
your loan, your
collateral, your house

00:37:44.845 --> 00:37:48.057
in a collateralized cash
deal, and the whole purpose

00:37:48.057 --> 00:37:50.559
of both the cash and the
collateralized cash deal

00:37:50.559 --> 00:37:53.521
is that it allows
the various actors

00:37:53.521 --> 00:37:55.731
to operate in derivative markets

00:37:55.731 --> 00:37:58.567
and make very high rates
of short-term return.

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:03.281
(wheels creaking)
(bell dinging)

00:38:03.281 --> 00:38:05.199
- Today, debt is
treated like an asset.

00:38:05.199 --> 00:38:07.451
It can be traded
globally, right?

00:38:07.451 --> 00:38:11.080
So we made a huge jump from
let's say the 12th century

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:14.208
when the first assignable
notes came about,

00:38:14.208 --> 00:38:16.585
to treating debt as an asset

00:38:16.585 --> 00:38:19.922
that we can just trade
on a global market.

00:38:24.260 --> 00:38:25.219
(slurping beverage)

