It is also not going
to finance and fund

00:38:46.615 --> 00:38:49.744
climate change mitigation
and de-carbonization.

00:38:49.744 --> 00:38:52.079
Money creates more money.

00:38:52.079 --> 00:38:53.789
Wealth creates more wealth.

00:38:53.789 --> 00:38:56.000
And this is a circle,
and it just rolls on.

00:38:56.792 --> 00:38:59.128
At the end of the
day, it's our houses,

00:38:59.128 --> 00:39:02.340
the value of our houses,
which are backing this up.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:04.925
The IMF has called it "the
shadow banking system."

00:39:06.093 --> 00:39:08.429
"Shadow" here doesn't
mean that it is illegal.

00:39:08.429 --> 00:39:11.515
It also doesn't mean that it
is necessarily shady and so on.

00:39:11.515 --> 00:39:15.686
It is just that it is shadow
because there are no rules.

00:39:15.686 --> 00:39:17.480
It's hardly any rules.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:21.776
It's sort of almost the Wild
West of the financial sector

00:39:21.776 --> 00:39:25.196
(dramatic Western music)

00:39:26.781 --> 00:39:28.783
- And a problem with
the derivative market

00:39:28.783 --> 00:39:29.992
is there is no market.

00:39:29.992 --> 00:39:32.328
So it's like one-on-one
swaps between bankers.

00:39:32.328 --> 00:39:33.788
It's like you and I say,

00:39:33.788 --> 00:39:35.164
"Hey, I know what bank
you're working for,

00:39:35.164 --> 00:39:37.458
"and so I've got some bonds,

00:39:37.458 --> 00:39:39.835
"and I want you to
insure 'em for me

00:39:39.835 --> 00:39:41.504
"in case the interest
rate changes.

00:39:41.504 --> 00:39:43.631
"Would you insure
these bonds for me?

00:39:43.631 --> 00:39:44.673
"How much would you charge?"

00:39:44.673 --> 00:39:45.633
You tell me a price.

00:39:45.633 --> 00:39:47.676
I say, "That's a good deal.

00:39:47.676 --> 00:39:51.472
"I'll get a bonus for
setting up the insurance.

00:39:51.472 --> 00:39:52.848
"You'll get a bonus

00:39:52.848 --> 00:39:55.184
"for taking in the money
to do the insurance."

00:39:55.184 --> 00:39:56.685
- But I work at
a different bank.

00:39:56.685 --> 00:39:57.978
- It's a good deal.

00:39:57.978 --> 00:39:59.188
- And I heard you
just bought those.

00:39:59.188 --> 00:40:01.023
And I figure I could
also get those,

00:40:01.023 --> 00:40:02.983
'cause I know a guy in
Saudi Arabia or China

00:40:02.983 --> 00:40:05.319
that would pay two cents more,

00:40:05.319 --> 00:40:06.821
and I could make
50 grand off it.

00:40:06.821 --> 00:40:09.240
- You wind up so far
from the underlying.

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:11.659
- The thing is, it's like the
old insurance principle, it's

00:40:11.659 --> 00:40:14.578
"How many people can insure
one person's house"?

00:40:14.578 --> 00:40:18.541
Can each person in
the neighborhood put
a $100,000 policy

00:40:18.541 --> 00:40:20.709
on that house and each
person collect $100,000

00:40:20.709 --> 00:40:23.337
when the $100,000
house burns down?

00:40:23.337 --> 00:40:24.880
(explosion roars)

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:26.715
(fire crackling)

00:40:26.715 --> 00:40:29.343
(explosion booming)

00:40:29.343 --> 00:40:30.845
- As Americans,
we're shortsighted,

00:40:30.845 --> 00:40:32.388
and we don't think long-term

00:40:32.388 --> 00:40:34.390
and financial literacy
was not this complicated

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:36.392
100 years ago. You had
a little bit of money,

00:40:36.392 --> 00:40:37.852
and probably didn't even
have a bank account.

00:40:37.852 --> 00:40:39.395
You paid your bills, and
you passed bills around.

00:40:39.395 --> 00:40:40.729
- And you kept it
under the mattress.

00:40:40.729 --> 00:40:42.064
- Right, or you kept
it under the mattress.

00:40:42.064 --> 00:40:43.065
Shh, that's not where mine's at.

00:40:43.274 --> 00:40:46.694
- The critical derivatives for
our financial markets today

00:40:46.694 --> 00:40:50.030
are actually the ones
that brought down
the financial system

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:53.284
in 2008 were the
credit default swaps

00:40:53.284 --> 00:40:55.703
and the collateralized
debt obligations.

00:40:55.703 --> 00:40:58.038
And the credit default
swap is basically

00:40:58.038 --> 00:41:00.916
a kind of an insurance contract,

00:41:00.916 --> 00:41:04.086
which basically says, "If
some assets in the markets

00:41:04.086 --> 00:41:08.424
"decline in value, the
insurer has to pay me a fee."

00:41:08.424 --> 00:41:11.552
And the beauty of the CDS,
the credit default swap,

00:41:11.552 --> 00:41:15.473
was that you didn't have
to hold the asset yourself.

00:41:15.473 --> 00:41:17.933
(fire crackling)

00:41:17.933 --> 00:41:19.226
It's like me taking out

00:41:19.226 --> 00:41:21.937
a fire insurance on
my neighbor's house,

00:41:21.937 --> 00:41:24.773
and if that house burns
down, I get the premium.

00:41:24.773 --> 00:41:27.735
- The value of these
financial transactions

00:41:27.735 --> 00:41:32.281
is much, much larger than
the tangible, real capital,

00:41:32.281 --> 00:41:34.742
which is basically the
houses of the families,

00:41:34.742 --> 00:41:36.744
the households,
the notional value

00:41:36.744 --> 00:41:39.622
of the derivative
market is $600 trillion.

00:41:40.414 --> 00:41:42.958
And that is about let's say,

00:41:42.958 --> 00:41:45.628
eight to 10 times as
large as global GDP.

00:41:53.469 --> 00:41:55.262
- So an American city with

00:41:55.262 --> 00:41:56.805
how many trillions of dollars

00:41:56.805 --> 00:41:58.849
of real estate
value right there,

00:41:58.849 --> 00:42:00.643
or at least hundreds
of billions,

00:42:00.643 --> 00:42:02.311
will no longer be there.

00:42:02.311 --> 00:42:03.604
Like, how does that work?

00:42:03.604 --> 00:42:04.980
How does that work in
the banking sector?

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:06.440
How does that work in
the insurance market,

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:07.816
the re-insurance market,

00:42:10.152 --> 00:42:12.655
what's been collateralized
off that real estate?

00:42:12.655 --> 00:42:15.449
- This is not just
different actors,

00:42:15.449 --> 00:42:17.451
exchanging in different markets

00:42:17.451 --> 00:42:20.829
or in different
legal transactions,

00:42:20.829 --> 00:42:25.793
our financial system is such
that we have huge banks,

00:42:25.793 --> 00:42:28.963
mega banks, too
big to fail banks,

00:42:28.963 --> 00:42:33.842
very often operating both
sides of the same market.

00:42:33.842 --> 00:42:37.846
- The big banks, they don't
necessarily know how much risk

00:42:37.846 --> 00:42:41.016
their counterparty bank
in some other country

00:42:41.016 --> 00:42:42.518
is willing to undertake.
- No one really

00:42:42.518 --> 00:42:45.062
understands the legal
interdependencies

00:42:45.062 --> 00:42:47.147
that are built into
these contracts,

00:42:47.147 --> 00:42:48.857
and that can
ultimately bring down

00:42:48.857 --> 00:42:50.484
the entire financial system.

00:42:50.484 --> 00:42:54.154
- The system is so complicated

00:42:54.154 --> 00:42:56.740
that the accountability is lost.

00:42:56.740 --> 00:42:58.993
- So the real value
is actually here.

00:42:59.034 --> 00:43:00.494
These are all the transactions

00:43:00.494 --> 00:43:03.247
which are built on top
of the real economy.

00:43:05.833 --> 00:43:08.252
(soft music)

00:43:35.696 --> 00:43:37.865
- Then there's Dougherty.

00:43:37.865 --> 00:43:40.284
The small town of
Dougherty is not,

00:43:40.284 --> 00:43:42.036
not a whole lot there today.

00:43:46.582 --> 00:43:50.878
Just it was a town that
once had a general store

00:43:50.878 --> 00:43:53.255
when I was growing
up, had the church,

00:43:55.549 --> 00:43:58.469
had a post office, but
you know, not anymore.

00:44:03.557 --> 00:44:06.602
- Agriculture and rural
America has gotten a preview

00:44:06.602 --> 00:44:07.978
of the automation
that's about to hit

00:44:07.978 --> 00:44:11.106
the rest of the country
in the next 10 years.

00:44:11.106 --> 00:44:14.735
- We're here at the town
square in Dewitt, Arkansas.

00:44:14.735 --> 00:44:16.820
And if you look around, I mean

00:44:16.820 --> 00:44:19.907
there's not a ton of
bustling businesses here,

00:44:19.907 --> 00:44:21.575
but one of the
busiest businesses

00:44:21.575 --> 00:44:23.285
is Department of Human Services.

00:44:23.619 --> 00:44:25.079
(soft music)

00:44:25.287 --> 00:44:29.917
So when we define the real
economy, let's not forget farms.

00:44:29.917 --> 00:44:33.462
88% are still
small family farms.

00:44:33.462 --> 00:44:36.632
- So this is a grain storage bin

00:44:36.632 --> 00:44:39.968
on my parents' family farm.

00:44:39.968 --> 00:44:42.429
(soft music)

00:44:49.436 --> 00:44:51.772
- This is my dad's place.

00:44:52.606 --> 00:44:55.943
He's gonna be 87, mom's 85.

00:44:55.943 --> 00:44:58.445
They've lived here since 1956.

00:45:01.281 --> 00:45:06.787
So, a lot of years.

00:45:08.789 --> 00:45:10.332
- And we are a team.

00:45:14.336 --> 00:45:17.715
- How many times you have
to have intense rains,

00:45:17.715 --> 00:45:21.510
heat spells, freezings at the
wrong point to get the crops

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:23.804
to not come in once?

00:45:23.804 --> 00:45:26.890
- We do protect our farmers,
we give them crop insurance

00:45:26.890 --> 00:45:29.184
and the crop insurance
is subsidized.

00:45:29.184 --> 00:45:31.812
We have agricultural
extension services,

00:45:31.812 --> 00:45:33.647
and they pay for that, but
they probably don't pay

00:45:33.647 --> 00:45:34.732
the full cost of that.

00:45:35.691 --> 00:45:37.693
It's a free market economy but,

00:45:37.693 --> 00:45:39.486
it's also a national
security issue.

00:45:45.993 --> 00:45:49.246
(birds chirping)

00:45:49.246 --> 00:45:50.664
- Federal crop insurance program

00:45:50.664 --> 00:45:53.333
was started as a
response to the effects

00:45:53.333 --> 00:45:55.753
of the dust bowl and
the Great Depression.

00:45:55.753 --> 00:45:58.422
Many, many areas throughout
the United States were,

00:45:58.422 --> 00:46:01.675
were severely affected
by the Dust Bowl,

00:46:01.675 --> 00:46:04.011
many farmers lost everything.

00:46:04.011 --> 00:46:06.013
In addition to most of America

00:46:06.013 --> 00:46:07.556
being devastated
by the depression,

00:46:07.556 --> 00:46:11.894
farmers acutely suffered
during that time.

00:46:13.771 --> 00:46:16.398
(somber music)

00:46:22.571 --> 00:46:24.281
Many parts of the plains

00:46:24.281 --> 00:46:27.576
were just completely
filled with dust.

00:46:27.576 --> 00:46:31.038
Many crops were lost
due to the weather,

00:46:31.038 --> 00:46:33.540
the extreme weather,
due to insects.

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:39.213
(somber music)

00:46:49.223 --> 00:46:51.391
- But it's the large
scale like my dad said,

00:46:51.391 --> 00:46:54.436
the large scale events that
everybody kinda worries about,

00:46:54.436 --> 00:46:56.063
like a big drought.

00:47:01.568 --> 00:47:05.239
- I think that our
aquifer in some places

00:47:05.239 --> 00:47:09.952
is down to like 20% and
then a lot of places at 50%,

00:47:09.952 --> 00:47:12.454
and we're actually
using more water than

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:15.290
we have at any other time.

00:47:17.251 --> 00:47:19.628
We have our pumping plants in

00:47:19.628 --> 00:47:21.922
and we're building on canals

00:47:21.922 --> 00:47:24.007
and this is actually
the first phase.

00:47:24.007 --> 00:47:27.678
We're trying to get water
to this, this Indian bio

00:47:27.678 --> 00:47:32.683
and then we're gonna use that
for the main water supply.

00:47:33.642 --> 00:47:36.603
In Arkansas, we're gonna
have a drought event,

00:47:36.603 --> 00:47:38.105
every growing season.

00:47:39.648 --> 00:47:41.775
It doesn't matter,
if we just came

00:47:41.775 --> 00:47:43.861
like this last flood
that we had here,

00:47:43.861 --> 00:47:46.113
as soon as we came out
of that flood event,

00:47:46.113 --> 00:47:52.661
we were irrigating because our
soil is, it's not real deep.

00:47:52.828 --> 00:47:55.205
We have what they call a
hard pan underneath it,

00:47:55.205 --> 00:47:56.957
pretty close to the surface

00:47:56.957 --> 00:47:58.458
- Which helps with
the rice growing.

00:47:58.458 --> 00:47:59.793
- Yes, very helpful.

00:48:01.628 --> 00:48:04.006
It just gets more
expensive every day.

00:48:04.006 --> 00:48:05.507
We thought we were gonna have

00:48:05.507 --> 00:48:08.343
a lot of infrastructure
money maybe to come about,

00:48:08.343 --> 00:48:10.679
but it hasn't come about.

00:48:10.679 --> 00:48:12.806
- And its federal infrastructure
money that coming?

00:48:12.806 --> 00:48:15.309
- Well, the way that was
originally set up was that,

00:48:15.309 --> 00:48:21.523
the federal government was
gonna be responsible for 65%

00:48:21.523 --> 00:48:25.819
the state 10, and our
local district, 25.

00:48:25.819 --> 00:48:27.821
And there's a lot of
people benefit from this

00:48:27.821 --> 00:48:29.489
other than just the farmers,

00:48:29.489 --> 00:48:32.367
but that's, who's
got to pay for it

00:48:32.367 --> 00:48:33.744
'cause those are only the ones

00:48:33.744 --> 00:48:35.370
that's actually using the water.

00:48:37.205 --> 00:48:38.665
- And I'm assuming
we'll have to do

00:48:38.665 --> 00:48:40.375
a whole bunch of
stuff like that.

00:48:40.375 --> 00:48:44.838
As temperature rises and
rain patterns change.

00:48:44.838 --> 00:48:47.382
When every person we've
talked to about farming

00:48:47.382 --> 00:48:49.176
and different methods of farming

00:48:49.176 --> 00:48:54.181
whether it's till or no
till or organic or not.

00:48:54.181 --> 00:48:55.182
(tractor engine purring)

00:48:55.182 --> 00:48:56.767
Each piece of land is different.

00:48:57.559 --> 00:49:00.187
And each little
community is different.

00:49:00.187 --> 00:49:02.105
And the ecosystem
there is different.

00:49:02.105 --> 00:49:05.692
So how can a giant JP Morgan,

00:49:05.692 --> 00:49:09.196
how can they service
a community like this?

00:49:09.196 --> 00:49:12.199
- Well, and why
would they want to?
- Yeah.

00:49:12.199 --> 00:49:14.034
- That's the rest of the story.

00:49:14.034 --> 00:49:15.369
I mean, that's not
gonna be a profit center

00:49:15.369 --> 00:49:17.287
for them to come out
here and look at a man

00:49:17.287 --> 00:49:20.290
that's farming
2,000, 3,000 acres.

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:25.045
They're looking for
20, 50,000 acre farms

00:49:25.045 --> 00:49:28.882
to where they can justify
spending some time on them.

00:49:29.716 --> 00:49:32.761
- The relationships with the
banks they've gotten big too.

00:49:32.761 --> 00:49:34.388
But it used to be
these smaller banks

00:49:34.388 --> 00:49:39.267
and they knew your
dad and his dad.

00:49:39.267 --> 00:49:41.895
If you went to a farm sale and
"Hey I liked this tractor."

00:49:41.895 --> 00:49:44.898
and it was $27,000.

00:49:44.898 --> 00:49:47.109
You just call the bank
and say, "Hey, I need it."

00:49:47.109 --> 00:49:48.568
"Okay, it's in your account."

00:49:48.568 --> 00:49:51.071
(gentle foreboding music)

00:49:51.071 --> 00:49:55.575
- 60 years ago if you made a
crop, you made money period.

00:49:55.575 --> 00:49:58.829
If you made a crop,
you paid all your bills

00:49:58.829 --> 00:50:00.580
and you could send
your kids to school

00:50:00.580 --> 00:50:02.249
and you buy your groceries.

00:50:02.249 --> 00:50:03.500
You were okay.

00:50:04.126 --> 00:50:08.255
Today, you can make a
crop and lose your assets.

00:50:08.255 --> 00:50:11.508
(gentle ominous music)

00:50:13.135 --> 00:50:15.095
(tractor engine revving)

00:50:15.095 --> 00:50:18.682
- Everybody out here,
it's all family and,

00:50:21.435 --> 00:50:26.106
I mean, if you were 30
years ago when we did this

00:50:26.106 --> 00:50:31.319
we get all, a
combine was $35,000.

00:50:31.820 --> 00:50:37.325
And now a combine is $400,000.

00:50:37.492 --> 00:50:40.996
(light airy music)

00:50:40.996 --> 00:50:43.498
- When you're looking
at machines that,

00:50:43.498 --> 00:50:45.208
so by the time you
have your headers

00:50:45.208 --> 00:50:47.169
and your everything
to go with it

00:50:47.169 --> 00:50:50.130
being upward close to
half a million dollars.

00:50:52.132 --> 00:50:53.800
Where else is
someone else buying

00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:55.552
half a million dollar equipment

00:50:55.552 --> 00:50:58.388
and I'm barely making ends meet.

00:50:58.388 --> 00:51:01.808
(tractor engine revving)

00:51:02.809 --> 00:51:05.228
It gets more
challenging every year.

00:51:05.228 --> 00:51:11.526
- The cost of production
and agricultural costs

00:51:11.693 --> 00:51:15.363
or just to be a farmer
and even a small farmer

00:51:15.363 --> 00:51:17.032
are almost cost prohibitive

00:51:17.032 --> 00:51:20.869
unless your family has been
in farming for generations.

00:51:20.869 --> 00:51:24.206
- Thank God almighty for
such a good looking crop

00:51:24.998 --> 00:51:28.043
while others have
had to struggle.

00:51:28.043 --> 00:51:29.252
(light airy music)

00:51:29.252 --> 00:51:30.545
- Oh, here's some fellows.

00:51:30.545 --> 00:51:32.714
- Yeah, that's who
we're riding with.

00:51:32.714 --> 00:51:34.007
Y'all be riding with him.

00:51:34.007 --> 00:51:36.718
(phone ringing)

00:51:36.718 --> 00:51:38.720
(indistinct)

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:40.055
Alright, thanks Zach.

00:51:40.055 --> 00:51:41.348
See you there.

00:51:41.348 --> 00:51:44.392
(tractor engine revving)

00:51:44.392 --> 00:51:46.228
- Farms are similar
to owning a house.

00:51:46.228 --> 00:51:48.021
You've gotta clean the gutters.

00:51:48.021 --> 00:51:50.398
You've gotta sweep the sidewalk.

00:51:50.398 --> 00:51:51.775
And the same thing with a farm

00:51:51.775 --> 00:51:55.028
you've got certain maintenance
that have to be done

00:51:55.028 --> 00:51:57.531
on annual basis to keep
it in a working order.

00:51:59.407 --> 00:52:03.411
- Before automation, everything
in here is handwritten.

00:52:04.287 --> 00:52:06.790
- 24th, 1955.

00:52:06.790 --> 00:52:08.458
- Yep. And everyday...

00:52:08.458 --> 00:52:10.460
- And that's everything that
happened during that day

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:11.920
all the money that came in.

00:52:11.920 --> 00:52:13.713
- They made four loans.

00:52:13.713 --> 00:52:16.383
Like this is, these are the
loans that we made that day.

00:52:16.383 --> 00:52:21.263
And you see the size of
the loans; $150, $500.

00:52:21.263 --> 00:52:22.931
- The countryside across America

00:52:22.931 --> 00:52:25.308
not just in our rural community
but other rural communities

00:52:25.308 --> 00:52:28.228
used to have a lot of
small community banks

00:52:28.228 --> 00:52:32.065
that were very plugged
into their communities

00:52:32.065 --> 00:52:33.483
and to the people
that live there.

00:52:33.483 --> 00:52:34.734
And trying to help them

00:52:34.734 --> 00:52:36.820
grow their businesses
and their lives.

00:52:36.820 --> 00:52:40.574
So just afraid of what the
landscape holds going forward.

00:52:40.574 --> 00:52:42.075
You're just a number,

00:52:42.075 --> 00:52:44.119
you're CUSIP number
on a bond security

00:52:44.119 --> 00:52:46.121
and here's where you
mail your check now.

00:52:46.121 --> 00:52:49.416
- It's getting harder and
harder for those farmers

00:52:49.416 --> 00:52:52.836
to stay in business because
they cannot get the funding.

00:52:52.836 --> 00:52:57.007
Because with each year
of them incurring loss

00:52:57.007 --> 00:52:59.092
and then no hope of...

00:52:59.968 --> 00:53:02.512
The prices don't get
better the next year

00:53:02.512 --> 00:53:05.974
regardless of the type
of natural disasters

00:53:05.974 --> 00:53:07.434
and things that happen.

00:53:07.434 --> 00:53:09.311
And so the farmer gets
further and further behind

00:53:09.311 --> 00:53:11.813
and we've had several
farmers that are actually

00:53:11.813 --> 00:53:13.023
going out of business.

00:53:13.023 --> 00:53:14.274
They're having to
sell their equipment

00:53:14.274 --> 00:53:17.944
and turn the farm over
to someone else to farm.

00:53:17.944 --> 00:53:21.281
(gentle ominous music)

00:53:21.281 --> 00:53:24.117
- It's actually something
that I have no control over.

00:53:24.117 --> 00:53:25.619
And none of us around
in England, Arkansas

00:53:25.619 --> 00:53:27.287
have any control over.

00:53:30.457 --> 00:53:33.460
And at the last
one, like in 2008

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:34.961
when they were saying that,

00:53:34.961 --> 00:53:37.464
"The banks wouldn't have money
to loan to your farmers."

00:53:38.548 --> 00:53:40.967
And I had several
farmers that were just

00:53:40.967 --> 00:53:42.177
pulling their hair out.

00:53:42.177 --> 00:53:44.221
They were just, "Are you
gonna be able to loan me

00:53:44.221 --> 00:53:45.972
enough money to
finish this crop?"

00:53:45.972 --> 00:53:47.182
And I finally told him,

00:53:47.182 --> 00:53:48.683
"Just go home and
watch Andy Griffith

00:53:49.809 --> 00:53:52.896
and quit watching CNN and Fox."

00:53:52.896 --> 00:53:56.149
And said, "You know,
as far as I know

00:53:56.149 --> 00:53:59.027
we're gonna have the money and
we're gonna take care of you

00:53:59.819 --> 00:54:04.991
and you need to quit
watching all of those..."

00:54:05.533 --> 00:54:06.868
- Stuff to scare you.

00:54:06.868 --> 00:54:08.995
- Yeah. That you
have no control over.

00:54:08.995 --> 00:54:12.374
So a couple of weeks later,
he came back and said,

00:54:12.374 --> 00:54:13.708
"You know that was
the best advice

00:54:13.708 --> 00:54:16.086
I've gotten so far this year."

00:54:16.086 --> 00:54:19.089
- The rainfall amounts, we
don't get an inch anymore.

00:54:19.089 --> 00:54:22.050
It's either three
inches or nothing.

00:54:22.050 --> 00:54:23.510
Everything is extremes.

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:26.721
(gentle ominous music)

00:54:30.850 --> 00:54:34.854
(gentle foreboding music)

00:54:34.854 --> 00:54:39.359
- Many of those restrictions
on banks are being peeled back.

00:54:39.359 --> 00:54:41.528
So we still have
trading in derivatives.

00:54:41.528 --> 00:54:44.406
It's not, we don't
know what it is.

00:54:44.406 --> 00:54:47.575
There's hundreds of
trillions of dollars

00:54:47.575 --> 00:54:52.580
maybe 600, maybe 800 trillions
of dollars of derivatives

00:54:52.747 --> 00:54:57.377
on currency exchanges
that can't be covered.

00:54:57.377 --> 00:54:59.921
(gentle ominous music)

00:54:59.921 --> 00:55:01.381
- It's like after 1980s it was,

00:55:01.381 --> 00:55:03.258
"Oh, Gordon Gekko
is a great guy.

00:55:03.258 --> 00:55:04.884
He taught us greed is good."

00:55:04.884 --> 00:55:05.802
Look what it's gotten us.

00:55:05.802 --> 00:55:07.429
- It's a cycle in America.

00:55:07.429 --> 00:55:08.763
You have to create wealth.

00:55:08.763 --> 00:55:10.223
You can't create wealth

00:55:10.223 --> 00:55:12.976
by just taking it from
other people through taxes.

00:55:12.976 --> 00:55:14.978
People have to be
incentivized to create wealth.

00:55:15.061 --> 00:55:16.563
And this has been the great-
- Okay, but wait a minute-

00:55:16.563 --> 00:55:18.982
- This has been
the great virtues

00:55:18.982 --> 00:55:21.318
of Western democracy
for 300 years...

00:55:21.318 --> 00:55:23.945
- But it's also been the
massively misunderstood.

00:55:23.945 --> 00:55:27.782
So go back to the
post-civil war United States

00:55:27.782 --> 00:55:28.950
and the growth.

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:31.953
The growth was made off land

00:55:31.953 --> 00:55:34.789
that people were literally
killed and chased off of

00:55:34.789 --> 00:55:38.501
and they're like, "Hey man,
look at all this free land."

00:55:38.752 --> 00:55:40.920
And that's how money was made.

00:55:44.257 --> 00:55:47.302
- You have to resolve that we
can do better going forward.

00:55:49.095 --> 00:55:53.141
American capitalism can
be pretty heartless.

00:55:54.309 --> 00:55:55.977
If you look back at
the American Civil War

00:55:55.977 --> 00:55:58.104
and what happened
right afterwards,

00:55:58.104 --> 00:56:00.648
we industrialized America,
we went into the Gilded Age.

00:56:00.648 --> 00:56:03.485
People look at these
"robber barons" and said,

00:56:03.485 --> 00:56:04.861
"This can't last."

00:56:04.861 --> 00:56:07.447
(somber music)

00:56:08.782 --> 00:56:11.159
And we ended up in the
progressive movement.

00:56:13.328 --> 00:56:15.288
This is the age of Reagan.

00:56:15.288 --> 00:56:17.332
The Reagan era
was the relaxation

00:56:17.332 --> 00:56:19.000
of the administrative state.

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:23.838
It was the sort of, let
big firms get bigger.

00:56:23.838 --> 00:56:25.465
It's let's make money.

00:56:25.465 --> 00:56:26.716
Let's do it all with money.

00:56:26.716 --> 00:56:28.968
Let's let charities
take care of people.

00:56:28.968 --> 00:56:30.970
Forget about
government programs.

00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:33.348
And that's run since
Reagan was elected.

00:56:34.849 --> 00:56:37.018
- Thank you very much.

00:56:37.018 --> 00:56:38.520
- I was raised red.

00:56:38.520 --> 00:56:40.688
I was raised in a red family.

00:56:42.148 --> 00:56:44.192
I grew up in the '80s.

00:56:44.192 --> 00:56:47.195
(upbeat '80s music)

00:56:56.746 --> 00:56:59.499
Fell in love with Ronald Reagan.

00:56:59.499 --> 00:57:00.875
I idolized Reagan.

00:57:00.875 --> 00:57:04.087
I saw Reagan and I
said, "I want his job."

00:57:04.087 --> 00:57:08.174
In fact, my parents told me
I mailed him a dollar bill.

00:57:08.174 --> 00:57:12.262
I defaced the dollar bill
with my picture on it.

00:57:12.262 --> 00:57:15.849
- The maxim is,
Doveryai, no proveryai

00:57:15.849 --> 00:57:18.184
trust but verify.

00:57:18.184 --> 00:57:20.562
(audience laughing)

00:57:20.687 --> 00:57:22.689
- [Translator] (speaking
foreign language)

00:57:22.689 --> 00:57:24.858
- (speaking foreign language)

00:57:24.858 --> 00:57:27.026
- [Translator] You repeat
that at every meeting.

00:57:27.026 --> 00:57:28.611
(audience laughing)

00:57:28.611 --> 00:57:31.197
- But I wanted to
be in politics.

00:57:31.197 --> 00:57:34.576
I wanted to be the first woman,
president of United States.

00:57:34.576 --> 00:57:36.244
I thought it was fascinating.

00:57:36.244 --> 00:57:37.912
- We can leave our children

00:57:37.912 --> 00:57:42.083
with an unrepayable, massive
debt and shattered economy

00:57:42.083 --> 00:57:44.544
or we can leave them liberty

00:57:44.544 --> 00:57:46.546
in a land where every individual

00:57:46.546 --> 00:57:49.883
has the opportunity to be
whatever God intended us to be.

00:57:51.926 --> 00:57:54.095
- People like to say
economics is a science

00:57:54.095 --> 00:57:56.598
but it really follows politics.

00:57:56.598 --> 00:58:00.727
Keynes came out and looked at
it, he saw the political need.

00:58:00.727 --> 00:58:03.062
That's how we understood
macroeconomics.

00:58:03.062 --> 00:58:04.272
Friedman came out
and looked at it

00:58:04.272 --> 00:58:06.733
and he saw deficit spending

00:58:06.733 --> 00:58:10.278
and he saw government control
and he saw high taxes,

00:58:10.278 --> 00:58:11.779
and he said, there has
to be a better way.

00:58:11.779 --> 00:58:14.908
He reinvented
classical economics.

00:58:14.908 --> 00:58:17.076
They called it
Neoliberal Economics.

00:58:17.076 --> 00:58:20.163
- Quoted Milton
Friedman and said that,

00:58:20.163 --> 00:58:23.666
that how the economy
of the United States

00:58:23.666 --> 00:58:28.254
oughta be judged by the
number of imports that we have

00:58:28.254 --> 00:58:29.464
and not exports.

00:58:29.464 --> 00:58:32.258
And I thought maybe this
was a typo or something.

00:58:32.967 --> 00:58:37.096
And so I asked him, I said,
"I mean, are you for real?"

00:58:37.096 --> 00:58:40.308
- Trickle-down economics
starts in 1980.

00:58:40.308 --> 00:58:41.267
It's right around in there.

00:58:41.267 --> 00:58:42.977
Late '70s, we get into it.

00:58:42.977 --> 00:58:44.979
But the bottom line is,
has two parts to it.

00:58:44.979 --> 00:58:46.773
First fire the cops.

00:58:46.773 --> 00:58:49.108
Not the cops on Main Street,
the cops on Wall Street.

00:58:49.108 --> 00:58:50.276
Turn them loose.

00:58:50.276 --> 00:58:52.445
Let those banks do
whatever they wanna do.

00:58:52.445 --> 00:58:56.699
- And so here we are now, 40
years into the age of Reagan

00:58:56.699 --> 00:58:58.952
and people are looking
at what it's done, okay.

00:58:58.952 --> 00:59:00.954
It's made a lot of
people really wealthy.

00:59:01.329 --> 00:59:05.041
We won the Cold War
during that period.

00:59:05.041 --> 00:59:08.836
Not necessarily because of
that, but we won the Cold War.

00:59:08.836 --> 00:59:13.341
And America became
this supreme power.

00:59:13.341 --> 00:59:16.636
But for the people of America,
it hasn't been so good.

00:59:16.636 --> 00:59:18.555
- I know, their real
wages went down.

00:59:18.555 --> 00:59:21.474
- Real wages went down and
people in this country look back

00:59:21.474 --> 00:59:22.725
and they wanna make America

00:59:22.725 --> 00:59:24.644
as great as it was in the 1950s.

00:59:24.644 --> 00:59:27.313
That's when the marginal
tax rate was 91%.

00:59:27.313 --> 00:59:28.147
- [Man] I know.

00:59:30.650 --> 00:59:32.694
- Ronald Reagan, my
mother loved him.

00:59:32.694 --> 00:59:35.697
She watched him every
Sunday night on GE Theater.

00:59:35.697 --> 00:59:39.033
At the end, he would say,
"At General Electric,

00:59:39.033 --> 00:59:41.869
progress is our most
important product."

00:59:43.705 --> 00:59:45.665
- [Wesley] What would
wrong Reagan say,

00:59:45.665 --> 00:59:50.211
if you told him that GE spends
more money on stock buybacks

00:59:50.211 --> 00:59:52.171
than it does on R&amp;D?

00:59:52.171 --> 00:59:55.508
In 1950, stock
buybacks were illegal.

00:59:55.508 --> 00:59:58.386
They were considered
market manipulation.

00:59:59.345 --> 01:00:02.390
Now, investment analysts
look at that company and say

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:04.100
"A lot of companies
are really smart.

01:00:04.100 --> 01:00:05.893
I think their stocks
gonna continue to grow up

01:00:05.893 --> 01:00:10.023
because they've got a really
great stock buyback program."

01:00:10.023 --> 01:00:12.025
It does nothing for the economy.

01:00:16.362 --> 01:00:18.531
- The average American is...

01:00:18.531 --> 01:00:20.867
they're living in
the GDP of 1980.

01:00:20.867 --> 01:00:22.118
- Yeah.

01:00:22.118 --> 01:00:23.786
I mean, yeah, clearly
the world has changed,

01:00:23.786 --> 01:00:25.872
and the commodities you can buy

01:00:25.872 --> 01:00:27.081
are not the same commodities,

01:00:27.081 --> 01:00:28.708
but in terms of your
let's say average

01:00:28.708 --> 01:00:30.877
to the extent that we can
measure living standards,

01:00:30.877 --> 01:00:32.587
living standards
have not improved.

01:00:33.379 --> 01:00:36.549
Firms finance their investment
out of their profits,

01:00:36.549 --> 01:00:38.051
that's what people think.

01:00:38.051 --> 01:00:40.887
Now, if you look at the 500
biggest U.S corporations,

01:00:40.887 --> 01:00:44.265
50% of the profits
are paid as dividends,

01:00:44.265 --> 01:00:47.727
50% of the profits are
useful buying back stocks.

01:00:47.727 --> 01:00:49.812
All the profits
dissipate, are gone.

01:00:49.812 --> 01:00:52.065
So how do they finance
their investment?

01:00:52.065 --> 01:00:53.441
If they--
- Debt!

01:00:53.441 --> 01:00:54.400
- Debt.

01:00:55.777 --> 01:00:57.737
- [Newsman] The president
expressed surprise

01:00:57.737 --> 01:00:59.906
about the collapse on
the markets yesterday.

01:00:59.906 --> 01:01:03.493
He's still enduring popularity
as much to the economic boom

01:01:03.493 --> 01:01:06.579
that America has enjoyed
under his leadership.

01:01:06.579 --> 01:01:09.916
- More people are working
than ever before in history

01:01:09.916 --> 01:01:12.752
where our productivity is up

01:01:12.752 --> 01:01:15.755
so is our manufacturing
product up.

01:01:16.964 --> 01:01:20.927
There is no runaway inflation
as there has been in the past.

01:01:20.927 --> 01:01:24.806
So as I say, I don't
think anyone should panic

01:01:24.806 --> 01:01:28.101
because all the economic
indicators are solid.

01:01:30.269 --> 01:01:32.772
- [Newsman] But the warning
signs have long been there

01:01:32.772 --> 01:01:35.358
in the closed factories in
many American small towns.

01:01:36.109 --> 01:01:37.485
- I mean, there is this notion

01:01:37.485 --> 01:01:39.487
of billionaires
creating employment,

01:01:39.487 --> 01:01:42.657
and that is
trickle-down economics.

01:01:42.657 --> 01:01:44.784
The problem about that
is that in history,

01:01:44.784 --> 01:01:46.285
it has never happened.

01:01:46.285 --> 01:01:48.663
There is no such thing as
trickle-down economics.

01:01:48.663 --> 01:01:50.498
It simply doesn't exist.

01:01:50.498 --> 01:01:52.458
- [Man] It really
is voodoo economics.

01:01:55.795 --> 01:01:57.839
(bird cawing)

01:01:57.839 --> 01:02:00.174
- When you put the smartest
people in the world in finance

01:02:00.174 --> 01:02:01.342
which is what we've done

01:02:01.342 --> 01:02:03.803
for the last 25 years
in this country,

01:02:03.803 --> 01:02:07.849
you can't hold back
their imagination
and their creativity.

01:02:09.684 --> 01:02:11.811
- The speed and scale of
global economic change

01:02:11.811 --> 01:02:13.813
has overwhelmed the
national systems

01:02:13.813 --> 01:02:15.690
of rules and regulations.

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:17.567
So our first test
is to agree tougher

01:02:17.567 --> 01:02:21.154
and more transparent supervision
of banks, hedge funds

01:02:21.154 --> 01:02:23.865
and what is known as the
global shadow banking system.

01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:29.412
- And so when you
get right down to it,

01:02:29.412 --> 01:02:30.830
I'm a licensed banker.

01:02:30.830 --> 01:02:33.499
And so when the regulators
come to my bank,

01:02:33.499 --> 01:02:35.042
we're very simple little bank.

01:02:35.042 --> 01:02:37.378
They look for the SOP,
they look for due diligence

01:02:37.378 --> 01:02:39.088
on projects we've done.

01:02:39.088 --> 01:02:40.673
It's pretty easy.

01:02:40.673 --> 01:02:45.386
You go to a big bank, Wells
or JP Morgan or Goldman,

01:02:45.386 --> 01:02:46.888
and you're a regular like,

01:02:46.888 --> 01:02:49.557
"What is this? I see these
emails and... " I mean,

01:02:50.349 --> 01:02:52.393
they don't understand it because
they're not working in it.

01:02:52.393 --> 01:02:53.269
They didn't invent it.

01:02:53.394 --> 01:02:54.896
- Sure.
- It's like trying
to teach people

01:02:54.896 --> 01:02:57.023
a theory of quantum gravity

01:02:57.023 --> 01:02:59.066
when they've had a high
school physics course

01:02:59.066 --> 01:03:01.736
and they're inspecting
Stephen Hawking or something.

01:03:01.736 --> 01:03:04.906
(gentle music)

01:03:04.906 --> 01:03:06.741
- [Katharina] The earth is
like a bounded natural system

01:03:06.741 --> 01:03:09.076
that can't expand forever.

01:03:09.076 --> 01:03:10.536
The beauty of the legal code

01:03:10.536 --> 01:03:12.914
and derivatives are a
product of the legal code

01:03:12.914 --> 01:03:16.417
is that we can expand it
how far we want, right?

01:03:16.417 --> 01:03:18.085
So we always create
new types of assets,

01:03:18.085 --> 01:03:21.756
we create new types of
legal tricks in the end,

01:03:21.756 --> 01:03:24.217
and the two systems are
not easily compatible.

01:03:24.217 --> 01:03:26.886
(gentle music)

01:03:28.304 --> 01:03:30.223
- If you're very rich and
you gamble with your money

01:03:30.223 --> 01:03:32.058
and you lose it in Las Vegas,

01:03:32.058 --> 01:03:35.144
that is what you want
and it's your problem.

01:03:35.144 --> 01:03:39.607
But here, if they lose their
money here, it is our problem.

01:03:40.274 --> 01:03:41.901
- The market can't punish

01:03:41.901 --> 01:03:43.903
for things it doesn't
know anything about.

01:03:43.903 --> 01:03:46.113
- And they haven't
factored any of the risks

01:03:46.113 --> 01:03:48.783
that all this could change.

01:03:48.783 --> 01:03:51.160
- Natural disaster could
have major repercussions

01:03:51.160 --> 01:03:52.662
for our financial system,

01:03:52.662 --> 01:03:56.082
just because they will have
an effect on the asset values.

01:03:56.082 --> 01:03:57.250
- [Roosevelt] The new law

01:03:57.250 --> 01:04:00.419
allows the 12
federal reserve banks

01:04:00.419 --> 01:04:03.923
to issue additional
currency on good assets

01:04:03.923 --> 01:04:07.426
and thus the banks that
reopen will be able to meet

01:04:07.426 --> 01:04:09.512
every legitimate call.

01:04:09.512 --> 01:04:11.472
It is sound currency

01:04:11.472 --> 01:04:16.269
because it is backed
by actual good assets.

01:04:16.269 --> 01:04:18.813
(water gurgles)

01:04:18.813 --> 01:04:21.107
(sizzling)

01:04:22.149 --> 01:04:26.487
(helicopter engine revving)

01:04:26.487 --> 01:04:29.448
(gentle music)

01:04:29.448 --> 01:04:32.493
- And so what happens is
that there's an event.

01:04:32.493 --> 01:04:36.330
And then when the event
happens, then everybody wakes up

01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:39.834
and sadly there's what we
call in a financial system,

01:04:39.834 --> 01:04:44.005
contagion, and people
unload, they unload stocks,

01:04:44.005 --> 01:04:46.507
they try to get their
derivatives repaid.

01:04:46.507 --> 01:04:48.885
They want their insurance
policies cashed.

01:04:48.885 --> 01:04:50.845
And the market
starts to unravel.

01:04:51.512 --> 01:04:53.472
When the market
starts to go down,

01:04:53.472 --> 01:04:56.517
the plugs get pulled everywhere.

01:04:56.517 --> 01:04:58.978
And so what you're dealing
with in climate change,

01:04:58.978 --> 01:05:01.314
it's not only the direct impact,

01:05:01.314 --> 01:05:03.691
but also the possibility
of contagion.

01:05:03.691 --> 01:05:04.817
- Economic contagion.

01:05:04.817 --> 01:05:05.651
- Economic contagion.
- Financial.

01:05:05.651 --> 01:05:06.527
- Yep.

01:05:06.527 --> 01:05:07.820
(air blowing)

01:05:07.820 --> 01:05:09.363
- Those views are
merely forecasts,

01:05:09.363 --> 01:05:12.366
and as always will
evolve with the arrival

01:05:12.366 --> 01:05:13.534
of new information.

01:05:13.534 --> 01:05:14.827
(sirens blaring)

01:05:14.827 --> 01:05:16.245
- Mike, they're on the
bell, hold the bell.

01:05:16.245 --> 01:05:18.748
(indistinct) the bells.

01:05:18.748 --> 01:05:20.499
- We actually have made it clear

01:05:20.499 --> 01:05:23.377
that we will bail out
these financial actors.

01:05:23.377 --> 01:05:24.587
We will bail them.

01:05:24.587 --> 01:05:26.505
This cannot be sustained.

01:05:26.505 --> 01:05:28.591
- If it runs only because
the central banks go in

01:05:28.591 --> 01:05:30.343
and buy assets, right?

01:05:30.343 --> 01:05:31.886
Recently it was
proposed that the

01:05:31.886 --> 01:05:33.262
European Central Bank

01:05:33.262 --> 01:05:35.890
should also start buying
shares of companies.

01:05:35.890 --> 01:05:38.559
So not only debt, but shares.

01:05:38.559 --> 01:05:42.021
So we're effectively
socializing the obligation

01:05:42.021 --> 01:05:44.565
to create financial returns

01:05:44.565 --> 01:05:47.902
for the holders
of capital assets.

01:05:47.902 --> 01:05:50.613
And that can't go on forever.

01:05:50.613 --> 01:05:53.407
(cheerful music)

01:05:59.246 --> 01:06:01.374
- Even though every
politician says,

01:06:01.374 --> 01:06:04.085
"This is the most important
election in your lifetime."

01:06:05.044 --> 01:06:10.091
These forces aren't tamed or
unleashed by a single election.

01:06:10.883 --> 01:06:13.427
(audience cheering)

01:06:14.553 --> 01:06:16.430
- In the news in the
last couple of weeks,

01:06:16.430 --> 01:06:18.766
we've read about
the coronavirus.

01:06:18.766 --> 01:06:20.267
And dude, China's acting like

01:06:20.267 --> 01:06:22.228
this is (scoffs) like
zombie apocalypse.

01:06:22.228 --> 01:06:23.604
- I'm actually kind
of afraid of that

01:06:23.604 --> 01:06:25.147
corona virus, man.
- Yeah. No, I don't wanna get

01:06:25.147 --> 01:06:26.440
on a plane.

01:06:26.440 --> 01:06:28.067
Do we have a government
that's competent enough

01:06:28.067 --> 01:06:31.112
and organized enough to
handle that kind of response?

01:06:31.112 --> 01:06:33.114
- No, man. I mean,
we can't even handle

01:06:33.114 --> 01:06:35.449
like homelessness
in the city of LA.

01:06:35.449 --> 01:06:36.951
You know what I mean?

01:06:36.951 --> 01:06:39.453
Like we can't even figure out
how to properly fund anything,

01:06:39.453 --> 01:06:41.330
let alone a pandemic.

01:06:41.330 --> 01:06:44.000
(gentle music)

01:06:45.751 --> 01:06:47.586
- Americans live
under the illusion

01:06:47.586 --> 01:06:49.755
that they're incredibly
different from other people.

01:06:49.755 --> 01:06:52.675
When I was in college
just everything started

01:06:52.675 --> 01:06:54.677
to melt down in the Balkans,

01:06:54.677 --> 01:06:57.471
everyone's like, "These
people are savages.

01:06:57.471 --> 01:07:00.141
"They've been fighting each
other for hundreds-of-years."

01:07:00.141 --> 01:07:03.769
It's like they just came down
out of the trees or something.

01:07:03.769 --> 01:07:06.605
Then you're there
and you realize

01:07:06.605 --> 01:07:09.608
these people live in houses
that have electricity

01:07:09.608 --> 01:07:11.652
and two-car garages
and refrigerators

01:07:11.652 --> 01:07:13.654
and not only
children in college,

01:07:13.654 --> 01:07:16.323
but several generations that
have gone through college

01:07:16.323 --> 01:07:19.660
and they all spoke
the same language.

01:07:19.660 --> 01:07:22.329
(gentle music)

01:07:23.497 --> 01:07:24.874
And these people
(jet engine roaring)

01:07:24.874 --> 01:07:26.333
killed each other.

01:07:26.333 --> 01:07:31.297
They committed genocide,
250,000 killed, rape camps,

01:07:31.338 --> 01:07:34.550
concentration camps,
ethnic cleansing.

01:07:35.676 --> 01:07:39.638
- This was a plan to
ethnically cleanse Kosovo

01:07:39.638 --> 01:07:41.807
and get rid of the
Albanian population.

01:07:41.807 --> 01:07:43.350
Are we going to stand for that?

01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:46.812
Is France going to stand
for that? I don't think so.

01:07:46.812 --> 01:07:48.022
- [Interviewer]
Like many countries

01:07:48.022 --> 01:07:50.691
as a system starts
to break down the,

01:07:50.691 --> 01:07:53.319
in their case, it was
the communist system,

01:07:53.319 --> 01:07:55.529
and in our case, our
capitalist system

01:07:55.529 --> 01:07:57.198
is starting to break down

01:07:57.198 --> 01:08:01.994
and people turn on each other
because some leaders think

01:08:01.994 --> 01:08:05.873
"Everybody's upset right now
and I can motivate my group

01:08:05.873 --> 01:08:07.541
"to take stuff from
another group."

01:08:07.541 --> 01:08:09.335
- [Narrator] Europe has
not seen displaced people

01:08:09.335 --> 01:08:12.671
in these numbers since
the Second World War.

01:08:12.671 --> 01:08:15.049
- And those are the kinds of
things we have to worry about

01:08:15.049 --> 01:08:18.010
with climate change,
because it happens when,

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:20.012
resources start to get tight,

01:08:20.012 --> 01:08:23.432
and everybody's a
human, everybody's
prone to these things.

01:08:23.432 --> 01:08:26.227
- We've pre-cut boards that
we can put over the windows

01:08:26.227 --> 01:08:28.395
in the event that there are
looters and things like that

01:08:28.395 --> 01:08:29.605
to protect the house.

01:08:31.273 --> 01:08:32.566
Yeah. You saw what
you want right?

01:08:32.566 --> 01:08:34.276
- We knew they wanted.

01:08:34.276 --> 01:08:39.865
- The UN said just two weeks
ago, a billion within 30 years.

01:08:39.865 --> 01:08:42.576
- Okay. A billion displaced
people, a billion refugees

01:08:42.576 --> 01:08:43.953
because they can't grow their
food the way they used to

01:08:43.953 --> 01:08:46.372
because of the drought
or because of floods

01:08:46.372 --> 01:08:48.874
or because of
collapse of systems.

01:08:48.874 --> 01:08:50.251
- [Reporter] How much
worse is this gonna get

01:08:50.251 --> 01:08:51.377
before it gets better?

01:08:52.920 --> 01:08:54.255
- I think the general view

01:08:54.255 --> 01:08:56.799
is this is going to
continue deteriorating

01:08:56.799 --> 01:08:58.884
for the time being,

01:08:58.884 --> 01:09:01.095
the next rains are
a long while off.

01:09:01.095 --> 01:09:03.389
- For the world, this
is a very bad thing

01:09:03.389 --> 01:09:06.892
because it's billions
of people dislocated,

01:09:06.892 --> 01:09:09.478
massive economic consequences.

01:09:09.478 --> 01:09:11.647
(fireworks cracking)

01:09:11.647 --> 01:09:14.483
- Can you have a
society that's been

01:09:14.483 --> 01:09:17.653
as successful as this one
has in a material sense?

01:09:17.653 --> 01:09:19.405
And can it transform itself?

01:09:19.405 --> 01:09:22.324
You know, in the past empires
haven't been able to do that.

01:09:22.950 --> 01:09:24.910
What would these Romans say?

01:09:26.287 --> 01:09:27.621
Could they help us?

01:09:28.581 --> 01:09:29.665
- [Wes Jr.] Unlikely.

01:09:29.665 --> 01:09:30.749
- [Wesley] Unlikely.

01:09:30.749 --> 01:09:32.126
(gentle music)

01:09:32.251 --> 01:09:35.129
The heritage of
America is Roman.

01:09:35.129 --> 01:09:37.798
All the men who wrote their
constitution had studied

01:09:37.798 --> 01:09:39.925
the history of Rome,

01:09:39.925 --> 01:09:42.636
I mean, the Senate it's Roman.

01:09:42.636 --> 01:09:45.431
- And it's failing us just
like the Roman Senate failed.

01:09:45.431 --> 01:09:48.267
- You know, there was a
Greek philosopher who said

01:09:48.809 --> 01:09:51.645
that governments
go through cycles.

01:09:51.645 --> 01:09:55.482
They go from a
democracy to oligopoly

01:09:55.608 --> 01:10:00.779
to dictatorships
and they collapse.

01:10:00.779 --> 01:10:03.866
- I know we're talking
about Aristotle but..

01:10:03.866 --> 01:10:06.160
- Actually it wasn't Aristotle,
it was a different guy.

01:10:06.160 --> 01:10:07.620
- No, it was Aristotle.

01:10:07.620 --> 01:10:09.955
- No, it was maybe
it was Polybius,

01:10:09.955 --> 01:10:11.123
I forget who it was.

01:10:11.123 --> 01:10:14.293
- I'll bet you cash,
it's Aristotle.

01:10:14.293 --> 01:10:16.879
'cause we're in Vegas, I will
bet you cash it's Aristotle,

01:10:16.879 --> 01:10:19.965
because Aristotle came
up with the definitions

01:10:19.965 --> 01:10:21.842
of the three kinds
of governments

01:10:21.842 --> 01:10:23.802
and their alter egos.

01:10:23.802 --> 01:10:26.847
So you have a democracy
which when it goes bad,

01:10:26.847 --> 01:10:28.349
becomes a mobocracy.

01:10:29.475 --> 01:10:31.685
You have a monarchy,

01:10:31.685 --> 01:10:34.688
which when it goes
bad becomes a tyranny

01:10:34.688 --> 01:10:37.024
and you have an aristocracy,

01:10:37.024 --> 01:10:39.693
which when it goes bad
becomes an oligopoly.

01:10:43.530 --> 01:10:47.534
- He's building on the
work of a predecessor.

01:10:47.534 --> 01:10:49.036
What's the name of
the predecessor?

01:10:49.036 --> 01:10:50.996
We have to find that out.

01:10:50.996 --> 01:10:52.206
- [Wes Jr.] I don't
know, you tell me.

01:10:52.206 --> 01:10:53.374
- [Wesley] We'll find it.

01:10:53.374 --> 01:10:54.875
I don't know, I
have to look it up.

01:10:54.875 --> 01:10:57.670
- [Narrator] Wealth was
obtained largely through trade,

01:10:58.879 --> 01:11:00.714
into the warehouses
full of goods

01:11:00.714 --> 01:11:03.175
from all over the
empire and beyond.

01:11:03.884 --> 01:11:06.428
It was the wealthy few
rather than the many,

01:11:06.428 --> 01:11:08.180
who benefited from the riches

01:11:08.180 --> 01:11:10.224
and vast resources
of the empire.

01:11:11.517 --> 01:11:14.395
This imbalance and the
irresponsible behavior

01:11:14.395 --> 01:11:16.021
of public officials,

01:11:16.021 --> 01:11:19.400
would become major reasons
for Rome's eventual decline.

01:11:22.403 --> 01:11:24.571
- Okay, so dad
and I had this bet

01:11:24.571 --> 01:11:26.532
and I need you to
see if I'm right

01:11:26.532 --> 01:11:28.033
or he's right.
- Okay.

01:11:28.909 --> 01:11:30.286
- It's about Aristotle.

01:11:30.286 --> 01:11:31.870
- Oh boy.

01:11:31.870 --> 01:11:32.746
- I know, I know.

01:11:32.746 --> 01:11:34.081
(laughing)

01:11:34.081 --> 01:11:37.543
So I was like well,
Aristotle described,

01:11:37.543 --> 01:11:39.545
the death of democracy
and what happens

01:11:39.545 --> 01:11:42.131
to different forms of
government as they decay.

01:11:43.382 --> 01:11:45.968
(gentle music)

01:11:49.430 --> 01:11:53.892
(speaking in a foreign language)

01:12:43.108 --> 01:12:47.279
- Well you see Alessia, I know
from also from your studies,

01:12:47.279 --> 01:12:49.990
that the collapse
of the Roman Empire

01:12:49.990 --> 01:12:54.328
was mainly a huge, gigantic,

01:12:54.328 --> 01:12:58.499
enormous, humongous,
financial collapse.

01:12:58.499 --> 01:13:02.961
At some point, the emperors
had just run out of money.

01:13:04.671 --> 01:13:07.007
- There are lots of
different theories

01:13:07.007 --> 01:13:09.218
why the Roman
Empire did collapse.

01:13:09.218 --> 01:13:10.511
I think what we know now,

01:13:10.511 --> 01:13:13.514
is that there has been
a long economic decline

01:13:13.514 --> 01:13:15.015
before the Roman
Empire collapsed,

01:13:15.015 --> 01:13:17.684
before the German
hoards came down

01:13:17.684 --> 01:13:19.812
and conquered Italy,

01:13:19.812 --> 01:13:21.355
and conquered Rome,
ultimately, right?

01:13:21.355 --> 01:13:25.818
So the question is
what triggered the
long economic decline?

01:13:25.818 --> 01:13:29.321
- Disparity between
the rich and the poor.

01:13:29.321 --> 01:13:31.990
- Onistorus and Miliorus they...

01:13:31.990 --> 01:13:33.325
- Yes, you're
speaking Latin now.

01:13:33.325 --> 01:13:34.576
- Yeah, yeah Latin.

01:13:35.661 --> 01:13:37.663
- Massive amounts of inequality

01:13:37.663 --> 01:13:39.998
may not be politically
sustainable.

01:13:41.208 --> 01:13:44.503
Governments fund themselves
through either debt,

01:13:44.503 --> 01:13:45.712
or through taxation.

01:13:45.712 --> 01:13:47.214
In Rome you would basically go

01:13:47.214 --> 01:13:50.717
and try to squeeze
more returns out of

01:13:50.717 --> 01:13:52.177
provinces that you occupied,

01:13:52.177 --> 01:13:55.055
or you might launch another
war to get the returns,

01:13:55.055 --> 01:13:57.516
to feed Rome and
feed those who were

01:13:57.516 --> 01:13:59.184
running the system overall.

01:14:00.561 --> 01:14:02.563
And I think that's
the common denominator

01:14:02.563 --> 01:14:04.898
because we live in a quite
different system today, right?

01:14:04.898 --> 01:14:06.567
So today it's our central banks

01:14:06.567 --> 01:14:08.902
who are doing the
rescue operation.

01:14:08.902 --> 01:14:10.529
- One thing about the
financial markets,

01:14:10.529 --> 01:14:13.031
one of their real power is,

01:14:13.031 --> 01:14:16.410
if they can pull forward
action from the future

01:14:16.410 --> 01:14:18.912
to the present, if
there's credible policy,

01:14:18.912 --> 01:14:21.415
after all, most of
what central banks do

01:14:21.415 --> 01:14:22.958
is effectively that.

01:14:23.917 --> 01:14:25.919
- So big trees fall hard.

01:14:25.919 --> 01:14:28.213
The market can
adapt to anything,

01:14:28.213 --> 01:14:29.715
as long as there's clarity,

01:14:29.715 --> 01:14:32.134
transparency and honesty

01:14:32.134 --> 01:14:35.596
and I don't think
we're getting that.

01:14:35.888 --> 01:14:38.223
- There were a lot of things
that caused the problem,

01:14:38.223 --> 01:14:41.977
but, I mean, essentially,

01:14:41.977 --> 01:14:43.437
the structure of Rome

01:14:43.437 --> 01:14:45.898
held together for
a good long time,

01:14:45.898 --> 01:14:48.066
until finally it just ran out

01:14:48.066 --> 01:14:52.779
of economic wherewithal
and leadership.

01:14:52.779 --> 01:14:54.615
- Politicians were bought,

01:14:54.615 --> 01:14:57.618
what the founders of
our republic warned us

01:14:57.618 --> 01:15:00.287
about over and over that
if you get really wealthy

01:15:00.287 --> 01:15:03.081
and you lose your moral
character, it's over.

01:15:03.081 --> 01:15:04.791
- Well, that's where we are.

01:15:04.791 --> 01:15:07.586
- Because we are very
close to a moment

01:15:07.586 --> 01:15:12.591
in which we could
start slowly going down

01:15:12.591 --> 01:15:14.927
and if we are not very careful,

01:15:14.927 --> 01:15:17.804
we will go down faster and
faster and faster and faster.

01:15:17.804 --> 01:15:19.139
(exclaiming)

01:15:19.139 --> 01:15:20.307
Roller coaster.

01:15:20.307 --> 01:15:22.518
It will be a lot of fun, maybe.

01:15:23.143 --> 01:15:25.604
You know the
ancient malediction,

01:15:25.604 --> 01:15:29.441
may you live in
interesting times.

01:15:29.441 --> 01:15:31.777
(loud bang)

01:15:32.778 --> 01:15:36.782
- So, that's how
it ended last time.

01:15:39.785 --> 01:15:41.787
Except this time, it
won't just be barbarians,

01:15:41.787 --> 01:15:43.997
it'll be the planet
itself that's on fire.

01:15:43.997 --> 01:15:46.625
(fire roaring)

01:15:48.669 --> 01:15:50.629
- [Man] There's still
active fire in that area.

01:15:50.629 --> 01:15:53.507
Monitor the conditions
and know what you'll do

01:15:53.507 --> 01:15:55.968
if the fire threatens
(indistinct)

01:15:55.968 --> 01:15:58.387
- [Woman] In the smoky
aftermath of the worst start

01:15:58.387 --> 01:16:00.806
to fire season
anyone can remember,

01:16:00.806 --> 01:16:02.683
while some search for solutions,

01:16:02.683 --> 01:16:04.643
others want someone to blame.

01:16:04.643 --> 01:16:06.812
(gentle somber music)

01:16:06.812 --> 01:16:08.647
- Probably should take a
break and let it settle in,

01:16:08.647 --> 01:16:09.690
take an intermission.

01:16:12.734 --> 01:16:16.029
- Financial ingenuity has
been part of this system

01:16:16.029 --> 01:16:18.156
since the Dutch invented the...

01:16:18.156 --> 01:16:19.366
- Okay,

01:16:19.366 --> 01:16:20.659
- But a lot of it
- liability company

01:16:20.659 --> 01:16:22.536
- isn't financial
- and Lloyds of London.

01:16:22.536 --> 01:16:23.579
But dad, listen.

01:16:23.579 --> 01:16:25.038
- People get pretty...

01:16:25.038 --> 01:16:26.540
- Everybody thought Bernie
Madoff was a genius.

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:27.708
- Yeah.

01:16:27.708 --> 01:16:30.002
Foreign investors looked
at the United States

01:16:30.002 --> 01:16:34.047
and said, "the United
States they're the best.

01:16:34.047 --> 01:16:36.925
"That's the safest place in
the world I can put my money".

01:16:38.051 --> 01:16:39.219
- [Woman] I mean, we've
had, I don't know,

01:16:39.219 --> 01:16:41.513
five, six biblical-like events.

01:16:41.513 --> 01:16:42.848
We're continuing to have them,

01:16:42.848 --> 01:16:47.853
so I need to
question the validity

01:16:48.520 --> 01:16:52.524
of the value of, and viability

01:16:52.524 --> 01:16:54.276
of my land long-term.

01:16:54.276 --> 01:16:56.862
(upbeat music)

01:17:00.866 --> 01:17:02.743
- A little over seven years ago,

01:17:02.743 --> 01:17:05.078
Hurricane Sandy
blew through here,

01:17:05.078 --> 01:17:08.874
causing tens of billions of
dollars in property damage,

01:17:08.874 --> 01:17:13.879
and since then, New
York has built more,

01:17:13.879 --> 01:17:19.926
bigger, taller and
more expensive,
because we, the people,

01:17:20.052 --> 01:17:24.556
are on the hook for the
cost of the insurance

01:17:24.556 --> 01:17:27.601
if another hurricane comes
and hits New York City.

01:17:27.601 --> 01:17:30.145
(rain pouring)

01:17:31.563 --> 01:17:34.232
(metal banging)

01:17:35.442 --> 01:17:37.444
The National Flood
Insurance Program

01:17:37.444 --> 01:17:41.615
is a federally-run public
program of insurance.

01:17:41.615 --> 01:17:44.993
It's run by the Federal
Emergency Management Agency.

01:17:47.996 --> 01:17:50.457
It provides flood insurance
for virtually all homeowners

01:17:50.457 --> 01:17:52.584
and small businesses
in the country.

01:17:52.584 --> 01:17:54.252
So this is pretty
unusual, actually.

01:17:54.252 --> 01:17:56.129
Most other countries
have private markets

01:17:56.129 --> 01:17:58.757
for flood insurance,
but the U.S does not.

01:17:58.757 --> 01:18:01.134
(sirens blaring)

01:18:01.134 --> 01:18:04.471
- It's a supplementary
policy that is subsidized

01:18:04.471 --> 01:18:09.476
by the government, and so all
citizens are, to some extent,

01:18:09.476 --> 01:18:13.105
footing the bill for
coastal property owners.

01:18:15.649 --> 01:18:18.860
- In order to build a huge
condominium development

01:18:18.860 --> 01:18:20.821
in a flood plain in New
York City, you, of course,

01:18:20.821 --> 01:18:21.988
need a lot of capital.

01:18:22.948 --> 01:18:25.784
Municipal actors in New York
City are very interested

01:18:25.784 --> 01:18:30.038
in continuing to promote a
version of New York City,

01:18:30.038 --> 01:18:34.668
in which as much of the
available land remains viable

01:18:34.668 --> 01:18:36.169
for continued
economic development,

01:18:36.169 --> 01:18:40.006
because property taxes are so
important there, as elsewhere.

01:18:43.468 --> 01:18:45.345
- Well, the original program

01:18:45.345 --> 01:18:46.972
of the National Flood
Insurance Program

01:18:46.972 --> 01:18:50.559
was supposed to stop
construction in very
high risk areas.

01:18:50.642 --> 01:18:52.728
Areas where water
moves with velocity,

01:18:52.728 --> 01:18:55.021
particularly flood
ways along the river,

01:18:55.021 --> 01:18:58.024
where water moves the
same rate as the river,

01:18:58.024 --> 01:19:01.653
hurricane-zoned areas, where
the storm surge comes in.

01:19:01.653 --> 01:19:03.655
The building is,

01:19:03.655 --> 01:19:06.533
it's almost impossible to
build a really safe building

01:19:06.533 --> 01:19:09.745
against a 14-foot wall
of water coming in at you

01:19:09.745 --> 01:19:11.413
in a storm surge.

01:19:12.998 --> 01:19:15.500
There's a lot of vested
interests that don't care,

01:19:15.500 --> 01:19:18.253
construction
industry, for example.

01:19:18.253 --> 01:19:22.758
They'd like to build
a house on a beach

01:19:22.758 --> 01:19:24.885
while the tide is out, as
long as they can sell it

01:19:24.885 --> 01:19:26.887
before the tide comes back in.

01:19:26.887 --> 01:19:29.598
(water flapping)

01:19:31.224 --> 01:19:32.851
And once they've
sold it, of course,

01:19:32.851 --> 01:19:34.728
the risk is now somebody else's.

01:19:35.562 --> 01:19:39.524
- The ways in which financial
actors securitized mortgages

01:19:39.524 --> 01:19:43.737
in the run-up to the
last financial crisis

01:19:43.737 --> 01:19:44.946
is still going on.

01:19:44.946 --> 01:19:46.782
- So the building
contractors all want

01:19:46.782 --> 01:19:49.451
to minimize the flood program.

01:19:49.451 --> 01:19:51.453
The town wants to minimize it,

01:19:51.453 --> 01:19:54.039
wanna hold down the
elevations on the maps

01:19:54.039 --> 01:19:56.208
and fight that every
map that comes out

01:19:56.208 --> 01:19:57.793
by the National Flood
Insurance Program,

01:19:57.793 --> 01:20:01.588
that causes problems,
delay in implementation,

01:20:01.588 --> 01:20:03.256
maybe never implementation.

01:20:04.466 --> 01:20:05.967
- The coastal communities

01:20:05.967 --> 01:20:09.054
are on some of the most
valuable land in the country,

01:20:09.054 --> 01:20:11.973
And as a consequence,
people are gonna fight

01:20:11.973 --> 01:20:15.560
to protect their
investment position.

01:20:16.436 --> 01:20:18.980
- I thought, okay, the
city's not requiring me

01:20:18.980 --> 01:20:21.149
to have flood insurance.

01:20:21.149 --> 01:20:24.402
My realtor didn't tell me I
had to have flood insurance.

01:20:24.402 --> 01:20:26.655
- Honestly, you don't
look at the flood maps

01:20:26.655 --> 01:20:28.615
when you move into
a neighborhood.

01:20:28.615 --> 01:20:32.410
You fall in love with the
house, and 85% of the people

01:20:32.410 --> 01:20:34.246
in my neighborhood
were not insured.

01:20:34.246 --> 01:20:35.956
- [Narrator] Shelter.

01:20:35.956 --> 01:20:38.959
- The United States
is uniquely vulnerable

01:20:38.959 --> 01:20:43.463
to building into
disaster-likely,

01:20:43.463 --> 01:20:47.676
physically vulnerable areas,
because of our reliance

01:20:47.676 --> 01:20:52.138
on local rule, particularly
for zoning decisions.

01:20:52.138 --> 01:20:55.767
That's part of our bedrock
of the United States,

01:20:55.767 --> 01:20:57.811
state rights and local rights,

01:20:57.811 --> 01:21:00.814
and it's actually has
a lot of advantages,

01:21:00.814 --> 01:21:02.983
but it has a major disadvantage,

01:21:02.983 --> 01:21:07.946
which is that local
governments depend on revenues

01:21:07.946 --> 01:21:11.157
that are coming from their
buildings, from their residents

01:21:11.157 --> 01:21:14.995
who are building and
living in their localities,

01:21:14.995 --> 01:21:20.000
and that means that
all residents benefit
from development.

01:21:23.837 --> 01:21:28.049
- The politics of provision
are so tied up with land values

01:21:28.049 --> 01:21:31.303
in the United States, that
if property values go down,

01:21:31.303 --> 01:21:34.973
then the tax assessments
adjust to reflect that,

01:21:34.973 --> 01:21:37.183
and you're bringing in less
revenue for the tax base,

01:21:37.183 --> 01:21:40.061
which means less investment
in local amenities

01:21:40.061 --> 01:21:43.189
and schools and local
public transportation.

01:21:45.859 --> 01:21:50.363
- In Texas, we don't
have state income tax.

01:21:51.364 --> 01:21:52.991
That's another reason
why you wanna live here,

01:21:52.991 --> 01:21:55.869
because it's cheap, but people
are taxed on their land.

01:21:58.204 --> 01:22:01.708
- The maps that are in
existence for where a 100

01:22:01.708 --> 01:22:05.003
or 500-year flood will
happen are so outdated

01:22:05.003 --> 01:22:09.257
and no longer accurate, the
risk and the cost of that risk,

01:22:09.257 --> 01:22:14.012
even within the insurance
industry, is not as accurate

01:22:14.012 --> 01:22:16.556
as it needs to be, in
order for us to appreciate

01:22:16.556 --> 01:22:18.016
what the cost of climate is.

01:22:19.392 --> 01:22:22.395
- Insurance actors
have found ways

01:22:22.395 --> 01:22:26.024
to take an insurance
policy and securitize that,

01:22:26.024 --> 01:22:28.026
and transfer it to kind
of capital risk markets,

01:22:28.026 --> 01:22:30.111
so they're not really
holding the bag,

01:22:30.111 --> 01:22:32.864
which means that they're
willing to underwrite more

01:22:32.864 --> 01:22:34.366
and more property,

01:22:34.366 --> 01:22:36.368
which means that people
can kinda continue to build

01:22:36.368 --> 01:22:37.577
in risky areas.

01:22:38.578 --> 01:22:41.206
- I don't think anyone
would wanna come in

01:22:41.206 --> 01:22:44.542
and look at the house and
come through salt water

01:22:44.542 --> 01:22:46.878
and wanna buy it.

01:22:46.878 --> 01:22:49.923
(water splashing)

01:22:49.923 --> 01:22:51.758
- This is my nest egg.
- Yeah, this is your nest egg.

01:22:51.758 --> 01:22:53.802
- This is how I'm gonna help
my kids and my retirement.

01:22:53.802 --> 01:22:56.388
- But now, because you
bought it in an area

01:22:56.388 --> 01:22:58.556
where there's rising sea level,

01:22:58.556 --> 01:23:00.767
maybe that nest
egg's not so secure.

01:23:00.767 --> 01:23:05.063
In order to convert
his nest egg to cash,

01:23:05.063 --> 01:23:08.108
he's gotta be able
to sell the house,

01:23:08.108 --> 01:23:12.404
but now nobody will insure
the next 30 year mortgage.

01:23:12.404 --> 01:23:14.406
What happens to John?

01:23:14.406 --> 01:23:16.282
- [Wesley] John's lost
his retirement nest egg.

01:23:16.282 --> 01:23:21.079
- Okay, so John is now
literally John on the street.

01:23:21.913 --> 01:23:26.001
- [Wesley] The nice house
that we're gonna buy

01:23:26.001 --> 01:23:27.669
in Florida to retire...

01:23:28.461 --> 01:23:30.130
- This was their
house in Florida.

01:23:30.130 --> 01:23:31.589
They were gonna move to Alabama.

01:23:31.589 --> 01:23:33.800
- [Wesley] Okay, well,
they're not moving.

01:23:33.800 --> 01:23:35.677
They may still move to Alabama.

01:23:35.677 --> 01:23:37.470
They may be living
in a trailer park.

01:23:39.431 --> 01:23:40.932
- Flood involves
the U.S government

01:23:40.932 --> 01:23:42.475
in this really peculiar way,

01:23:42.475 --> 01:23:46.271
fire is privately underwritten,

01:23:46.271 --> 01:23:47.981
so it's private insurers

01:23:47.981 --> 01:23:52.610
and what's happening
with fire risk,

01:23:52.610 --> 01:23:54.821
is that some of the
primary insurers

01:23:54.821 --> 01:23:56.114
are going out of business,

01:23:56.114 --> 01:23:57.782
in events like the
California wildfires,

01:23:57.782 --> 01:23:59.159
that can put an insurer
out of business,

01:23:59.159 --> 01:24:01.786
if they have
outstanding liabilities

01:24:01.786 --> 01:24:03.663
that exceed what
they can pay off.

01:24:06.499 --> 01:24:07.876
- [Wes Jr.] Then
we started talking

01:24:07.876 --> 01:24:10.503
and you told me
about buying a house

01:24:10.503 --> 01:24:11.838
in Sausalito.

01:24:11.838 --> 01:24:13.173
So what happened?

01:24:13.173 --> 01:24:14.883
- So we call the insurance
company two days before,

01:24:14.883 --> 01:24:16.384
two or three days before

01:24:16.384 --> 01:24:17.802
and let them know we're
gonna close in a few days.

01:24:17.802 --> 01:24:19.012
And they call us back and say,

01:24:19.012 --> 01:24:20.221
" Hey, we can't
insure that house"

01:24:20.221 --> 01:24:21.681
And I'm like, "what?"

01:24:21.681 --> 01:24:24.350
And they're like " yeah, it's
on a list of neighborhoods

01:24:24.350 --> 01:24:25.727
"that we don't insure anymore"

01:24:25.727 --> 01:24:29.022
So these entire neighborhoods
are gonna be uninsured

01:24:29.022 --> 01:24:30.190
and they also told us,

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:32.150
they don't know if
they're gonna reinsure

01:24:32.150 --> 01:24:34.069
the current owner
of the house either.

01:24:34.069 --> 01:24:35.904
That's an entire,

01:24:35.904 --> 01:24:39.324
billions of dollars of
houses across the state,

01:24:39.324 --> 01:24:41.409
that it's gonna have
an insurance problem.

01:24:42.577 --> 01:24:46.206
(jet engine roaring)

01:24:46.206 --> 01:24:49.501
- The insurance companies
are insuring for one year.

01:24:49.501 --> 01:24:51.753
So as things get worse,
the insurance companies

01:24:51.753 --> 01:24:54.172
are definitely not
going to be able

01:24:54.172 --> 01:24:56.508
to come to the rescue
of these homeowners.

01:24:57.675 --> 01:25:00.386
- These latent,
hidden climate risks

01:25:00.386 --> 01:25:03.681
that might exist in
different places,

01:25:03.681 --> 01:25:05.225
in different housing markets,

01:25:05.225 --> 01:25:07.519
should they burst,
could take down

01:25:07.519 --> 01:25:09.187
the entire financial system.

01:25:11.022 --> 01:25:15.902
- And right now this house
is listed for $49,000.

01:25:15.902 --> 01:25:19.739
At the peak of the market,
it went to about $250,000.

01:25:20.907 --> 01:25:23.243
- It's not only the owners
that lose their assets,

01:25:23.243 --> 01:25:25.078
it's the financial
intermediaries that lose

01:25:25.078 --> 01:25:26.704
their claims against them.

01:25:26.704 --> 01:25:29.082
It's those that bought
the secularized mortgages

01:25:29.082 --> 01:25:31.584
and other assets that
are linked to them,

01:25:31.584 --> 01:25:34.879
just as we've seen
in the 2008 crisis.

01:25:34.879 --> 01:25:37.382
- And unless we're prepared
to continue to write

01:25:37.382 --> 01:25:39.425
the same check over
and over again,

01:25:39.425 --> 01:25:41.427
we need to start to scrutinize,

01:25:42.220 --> 01:25:47.892
are we hardening the utilities
and the infrastructure

01:25:47.892 --> 01:25:50.228
around these homes sufficiently,

01:25:50.228 --> 01:25:53.106
so they can weather these
storms going forward?

01:25:53.106 --> 01:25:55.900
Or should we in fact not permit

01:25:55.900 --> 01:26:00.405
the investment of capital,
that is going to be subject to,

01:26:01.739 --> 01:26:05.285
climate change conditions
in a negative way

01:26:05.285 --> 01:26:06.786
and we need to think about that.

01:26:06.786 --> 01:26:09.581
- No, that's the
really scary thing

01:26:09.581 --> 01:26:12.458
because we're not, you
know we're not hardening

01:26:12.458 --> 01:26:14.127
any of our infrastructure.

01:26:14.127 --> 01:26:14.961
(laughing)

01:26:14.961 --> 01:26:16.087
I mean, not yet.

01:26:16.087 --> 01:26:16.963
It's very expensive.

01:26:16.963 --> 01:26:17.922
There's guys thinking about it,

01:26:17.922 --> 01:26:19.632
but nobody's done it yet.

01:26:19.632 --> 01:26:22.093
(train approaching)

01:26:22.093 --> 01:26:24.345
- Well, from an oil
company standpoint,

01:26:25.263 --> 01:26:28.516
most of the major assets
in the United States

01:26:28.516 --> 01:26:31.644
and actually globally,
are on the coastline.

01:26:34.272 --> 01:26:38.151
From that standpoint, the
facilities are as vulnerable

01:26:38.151 --> 01:26:40.028
as people's homes.

01:26:40.028 --> 01:26:42.322
So there's a lot of
offshore infrastructure

01:26:42.322 --> 01:26:43.990
in the Gulf of Mexico,

01:26:43.990 --> 01:26:47.327
but that comes on shore
to a lot of pipelines

01:26:47.327 --> 01:26:49.704
that are right at the coastline.

01:26:49.704 --> 01:26:52.290
And so as the coastline erodes,

01:26:52.290 --> 01:26:55.627
those pipelines become
noticeable and vulnerable

01:26:55.627 --> 01:26:59.881
and have to be buried deeper,

01:26:59.881 --> 01:27:03.134
or infrastructure put
around it to protect it.

01:27:03.843 --> 01:27:07.180
So indeed, yeah, hardening
of infrastructure,

01:27:07.180 --> 01:27:11.476
utilities and fires have
been associated with that,

01:27:11.476 --> 01:27:13.478
or the pipelines.

01:27:13.478 --> 01:27:17.065
People are worried about how
to protect those going forward.

01:27:17.982 --> 01:27:21.861
- Nature's gonna be
eroding the foundations

01:27:21.861 --> 01:27:23.154
of our economy.

01:27:23.154 --> 01:27:25.823
(waves roaring)

01:27:30.495 --> 01:27:34.082
- So, sometimes I'm out
in a place like this,

01:27:35.041 --> 01:27:37.669
and I imagine what it
would be like to come here

01:27:37.669 --> 01:27:40.088
with no money in my pocket,

01:27:41.381 --> 01:27:44.842
fleeing an economic meltdown,

01:27:44.842 --> 01:27:47.220
'cause you know look, we
do have plenty of land,

01:27:48.930 --> 01:27:51.391
but if I had to survive out here

01:27:51.391 --> 01:27:52.600
and didn't have the money

01:27:53.893 --> 01:27:56.396
to buy something to
provide me power,

01:27:57.438 --> 01:27:59.440
some way to get water,

01:27:59.440 --> 01:28:00.942
out of a fairly dry land,

01:28:02.193 --> 01:28:04.570
while we love pristine nature

01:28:04.570 --> 01:28:06.239
and we like to hike in it,

01:28:06.239 --> 01:28:09.200
hunt in it, camp in it,

01:28:09.200 --> 01:28:11.411
all those things we do

01:28:11.411 --> 01:28:16.082
thanks to the technology
that you bought at the store.

01:28:16.082 --> 01:28:20.712
The tent, the gun,
the flashlight,

01:28:20.712 --> 01:28:24.757
the lighter, these things are
made in human communities,

01:28:24.757 --> 01:28:28.094
in factories, not by
people on the run,

01:28:28.094 --> 01:28:29.804
not by people out of capital.

01:28:32.056 --> 01:28:34.642
(gentle music)

01:28:36.436 --> 01:28:37.895
It's all about capital.

01:28:41.274 --> 01:28:42.900
So here's what happens,

01:28:42.900 --> 01:28:45.945
capital is gonna flow to where
it can make the most money

01:28:45.945 --> 01:28:47.113
for the capital owner.

01:28:48.072 --> 01:28:49.282
A town like this,

01:28:49.282 --> 01:28:51.659
which was a silver mining town,

01:28:51.659 --> 01:28:54.954
is a good example
of foraging theory,

01:28:54.954 --> 01:28:57.165
where if you're an animal or,

01:28:57.749 --> 01:29:01.419
and these things translate
into the human world as well,

01:29:01.419 --> 01:29:03.254
and you've got a choice
between two things,

01:29:03.254 --> 01:29:04.797
one that's easy to get

01:29:04.797 --> 01:29:06.257
and one that's hard to get.

01:29:06.257 --> 01:29:08.843
If the easy thing to
get is worth more,

01:29:08.843 --> 01:29:10.470
that's what you're gonna go for.

01:29:10.470 --> 01:29:12.764
So that's what
produces boom towns,

01:29:12.764 --> 01:29:16.267
because at one point the silver
was really easy to get to

01:29:16.267 --> 01:29:17.435
and worth something

01:29:17.435 --> 01:29:19.645
and then when the
silver market crashed,

01:29:19.645 --> 01:29:21.481
well, it wasn't worth anything.

01:29:21.481 --> 01:29:26.194
So they moved on, to find the
next easiest thing to mine.

01:29:26.944 --> 01:29:32.158
These patterns of capital
do not change over time.

01:29:32.325 --> 01:29:36.037
Capital is always gonna
seek the greatest return

01:29:36.037 --> 01:29:39.207
and as soon as that
return isn't paying off,

01:29:39.207 --> 01:29:42.168
it's gonna pull up stakes
and go somewhere else.

01:29:42.627 --> 01:29:46.964
- The way to define
capital is the money

01:29:46.964 --> 01:29:51.177
that is going to be used
to create something.

01:29:51.177 --> 01:29:54.680
- People are very myopic.

01:29:54.680 --> 01:29:57.892
They are just looking at
what's in front of their face

01:29:57.892 --> 01:30:00.311
and the portfolio
that's right there,

01:30:00.311 --> 01:30:02.688
how they're gonna make
money in the short term.

01:30:09.153 --> 01:30:10.738
- How you doing Frank?

01:30:10.738 --> 01:30:11.906
(laughing)

01:30:11.906 --> 01:30:14.992
So look, we look
around and it's like,

01:30:14.992 --> 01:30:16.202
this is all the capital,

01:30:16.202 --> 01:30:17.703
this is all the
capital in the world,

01:30:17.703 --> 01:30:19.163
headquartered right here.

01:30:19.163 --> 01:30:20.498
- Right.

01:30:20.498 --> 01:30:24.502
- And I'm assuming they
all wanna live past 2050

01:30:24.502 --> 01:30:26.337
and so we gotta change over.

01:30:26.337 --> 01:30:31.509
Are they beating down a
trail to your door to invest?

01:30:31.509 --> 01:30:33.010
- No, they're not.

01:30:33.010 --> 01:30:36.389
We have to search for the
right type of investors

01:30:36.389 --> 01:30:38.224
for our projects.

01:30:38.224 --> 01:30:43.062
And when we do find the
investors for our projects,

01:30:43.062 --> 01:30:47.442
it's extremely difficult
to create the value

01:30:47.442 --> 01:30:51.362
or pull out the value for our
projects that the investors

01:30:51.362 --> 01:30:54.449
recognize immediately.

01:30:54.449 --> 01:30:59.412
- If somebody can get 15% on
their money doing derivatives,

01:30:59.412 --> 01:31:01.956
why would they wanna
get 5% on their money

01:31:01.956 --> 01:31:04.417
doing renewable?

01:31:04.417 --> 01:31:06.586
- Because they would be able

01:31:06.586 --> 01:31:09.589
to use the investment
tax credit.

01:31:09.589 --> 01:31:10.923
Everybody wants a return

01:31:10.923 --> 01:31:13.926
and everybody wants to
maximize their return.

01:31:13.926 --> 01:31:17.889
And the only groups that I
know can maximize their return

01:31:17.889 --> 01:31:20.558
for renewable energy
projects today,

01:31:20.558 --> 01:31:25.271
are the wealthy individuals
that have passive income.

01:31:25.271 --> 01:31:27.607
- And, what do we think of
as like wealthy individuals?

01:31:27.607 --> 01:31:30.902
So I think a lot of Americans
think if someone has $500,000,

01:31:30.902 --> 01:31:32.111
they are a wealthy individual.

01:31:32.111 --> 01:31:33.738
- I'm talking about
people who have

01:31:33.738 --> 01:31:36.240
hundreds and hundreds
of millions of
dollars of net worth.

01:31:36.240 --> 01:31:39.619
- So minimum, a hundred
million dollars of net worth.

01:31:39.619 --> 01:31:41.954
- I would say so, but
there's not many people

01:31:41.954 --> 01:31:44.790
maybe less than 1%
of the population

01:31:44.790 --> 01:31:47.418
that has that type of
investment portfolio,

01:31:47.418 --> 01:31:51.172
or investments generating
passive income.

01:31:51.797 --> 01:31:53.299
- Any more comments?

01:31:53.299 --> 01:31:55.301
- That's pretty close
all right, pretty close.

01:31:55.301 --> 01:31:56.135
- Perfect.

01:31:56.135 --> 01:31:57.094
- All right.

01:31:57.094 --> 01:31:58.846
- So when there's not investment

01:31:58.846 --> 01:32:01.933
in the market towards
the technology

01:32:01.933 --> 01:32:04.977
that would help for
climate solutions,

01:32:04.977 --> 01:32:07.313
and renewables and clean energy,

01:32:07.313 --> 01:32:10.316
then that trickles
down into the entire,

01:32:11.776 --> 01:32:15.321
the entire life
stream or life cycle,

01:32:15.321 --> 01:32:18.783
you know, the whole
flow of business.

01:32:18.783 --> 01:32:21.494
- Lord Keynes, who was the
founder of macro economics

01:32:21.494 --> 01:32:24.163
asked the question,
why do people invest?

01:32:24.163 --> 01:32:26.707
And honestly, there's no answer.

01:32:26.707 --> 01:32:29.377
He called it animal spirits.

01:32:31.337 --> 01:32:33.881
I can tell you people
are risk averse.

01:32:33.881 --> 01:32:37.301
And after 2008, they're
even more risk averse.

01:32:38.678 --> 01:32:40.555
When I go to these
business conferences

01:32:40.555 --> 01:32:42.348
and I see my friends,

01:32:42.348 --> 01:32:44.809
I asked the guy who's worth
a hundred million dollars,

01:32:44.809 --> 01:32:46.352
I said, what are
you working on now?

01:32:46.352 --> 01:32:48.312
He says, you know,
he said, I'm just,

01:32:48.312 --> 01:32:50.356
I'm pretty happy where I am

01:32:51.023 --> 01:32:54.527
and you know, I've got
the house in Mexico

01:32:54.527 --> 01:32:56.362
and we're looking
after the grandkids,

01:32:56.362 --> 01:32:59.490
and I mean, he's not
investing in taking risks.

01:32:59.490 --> 01:33:01.701
He's not creating jobs
for the rest of us,

01:33:01.701 --> 01:33:03.369
at least not directly.

01:33:03.369 --> 01:33:05.871
That's the animal spirits
that are out there

01:33:05.871 --> 01:33:08.666
in too much of the business
community right now.

01:33:08.666 --> 01:33:11.502
So the only way you
can replace that

01:33:11.502 --> 01:33:14.046
is with government leadership.

01:33:14.046 --> 01:33:17.341
The Erie Canal, the
Transcontinental Railroad,

01:33:17.341 --> 01:33:20.720
the Panama Canal,
the space program,

01:33:20.720 --> 01:33:23.889
the interstate highway system,

01:33:23.889 --> 01:33:27.518
in no case did a group of
entrepreneurs get together

01:33:27.518 --> 01:33:30.438
and say, hey let's build the
interstate highway system.

01:33:30.438 --> 01:33:34.900
No, it was a dream
that Ike pushed in 1955

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:36.736
that came from a study done

01:33:36.736 --> 01:33:39.113
during World War
II that showed that

01:33:39.113 --> 01:33:41.616
you needed these highways for
national security purposes.

01:33:41.616 --> 01:33:43.951
- This is no partisan policy.

01:33:43.951 --> 01:33:48.914
No one has a monopoly on truth,

01:33:48.914 --> 01:33:51.417
and on the facts that
affect this country,

01:33:51.417 --> 01:33:52.627
we must work together.

01:33:54.211 --> 01:33:56.380
- The United States is
highly dis-aggregated.

01:33:56.380 --> 01:33:58.924
We have 50 states, one nation,

01:33:58.924 --> 01:34:00.259
many counties, many regions,

01:34:00.259 --> 01:34:01.469
tens of thousands of cities

01:34:01.469 --> 01:34:03.429
they all have a voice
in how things are done.

01:34:03.429 --> 01:34:07.141
And it's hard to get us to
collaborate on certain projects,

01:34:07.141 --> 01:34:10.102
unless we all align and
agree it's necessary

01:34:10.102 --> 01:34:12.396
and we feel some
urgency to get it done.

01:34:14.273 --> 01:34:16.275
- If the Australian
military study,

01:34:16.275 --> 01:34:17.485
the U.S military study,

01:34:17.485 --> 01:34:19.737
and the British military
study are all correct

01:34:19.737 --> 01:34:21.989
we've got about 30 years

01:34:21.989 --> 01:34:25.242
if we don't do anything
until civilization collapses.

01:34:25.242 --> 01:34:28.287
Which I'm guessing doesn't
have a good rate of return.

01:34:28.287 --> 01:34:29.246
- Correct?

01:34:29.246 --> 01:34:31.123
So Wes there are actually those

01:34:32.249 --> 01:34:33.626
who would tell
you that we may be

01:34:33.626 --> 01:34:35.753
beyond the tipping
point already,

01:34:35.753 --> 01:34:38.255
I don't subscribe
to that, by the way.

01:34:38.964 --> 01:34:42.009
If you do subscribe to that then

01:34:42.009 --> 01:34:44.303
it almost makes
the investment in,

01:34:44.303 --> 01:34:47.807
you know wind and
solar, not pertinent.

01:34:55.106 --> 01:34:57.775
- [Wesley] What's the most
valuable thing in the world?

01:34:57.775 --> 01:34:59.777
The most valuable thing,

01:34:59.777 --> 01:35:02.988
it's oil reserves in the ground.

01:35:02.988 --> 01:35:06.033
It's worth more than the
GDP of the United States,

01:35:06.033 --> 01:35:08.285
more than the stock market,

01:35:08.285 --> 01:35:11.539
more than the stock markets
of all the Western countries,

01:35:11.539 --> 01:35:13.457
it's oil in the ground,

01:35:13.457 --> 01:35:18.462
and the people that have that
oil, they want it to be used.

01:35:18.838 --> 01:35:20.840
Whatever you hear
about Saudi Arabia

01:35:20.840 --> 01:35:22.299
saying they're
going to, you know

01:35:22.299 --> 01:35:25.678
move beyond a petrol
based economy, yell, yell,

01:35:25.678 --> 01:35:27.680
but they're going to
move at their pace

01:35:27.680 --> 01:35:30.516
and they need those petro
dollars to get there.

01:35:30.516 --> 01:35:34.562
The societal costs right
now are much greater

01:35:34.562 --> 01:35:38.065
than the economic costs of
taking it out of the ground.

01:35:38.065 --> 01:35:40.735
So you have to recognize
the societal impact

01:35:40.735 --> 01:35:42.194
and you have to make the people

01:35:42.194 --> 01:35:44.530
who are producing
it and selling it,

01:35:44.530 --> 01:35:46.907
and the people who are buying it

01:35:46.907 --> 01:35:50.703
pay the full cost of using it.

01:35:50.703 --> 01:35:52.663
And that, that means a tax.

01:35:52.663 --> 01:35:55.833
- But we didn't even do
that with security though,

01:35:55.833 --> 01:35:57.668
because I mean, realistically

01:35:57.668 --> 01:36:00.254
we've subsidized the oil
industry for 50 years

01:36:00.254 --> 01:36:02.381
by knocking over governments,

01:36:02.381 --> 01:36:05.551
by protecting shipping
lanes for it, oil lanes,

01:36:05.551 --> 01:36:08.220
so we couldn't even do
it when it came to like

01:36:09.680 --> 01:36:14.393
having them pay for their
own extraction and security.

01:36:14.393 --> 01:36:15.895
- It's true.

01:36:15.895 --> 01:36:18.189
But one of the reasons we
couldn't do it is because,

01:36:18.189 --> 01:36:20.691
we didn't really
have the alternative.

01:36:20.691 --> 01:36:22.359
- What's the sound?

01:36:22.359 --> 01:36:25.196
- We didn't really have the
alternative technically.

01:36:31.410 --> 01:36:34.580
(jet engine whirring)

01:36:37.875 --> 01:36:40.419
(fire blazing)

01:36:44.256 --> 01:36:46.133
- [Narrator 1] Engineers
worked under fire

01:36:46.133 --> 01:36:48.886
to douse oil
pipelines set ablaze

01:36:48.886 --> 01:36:50.429
by retreating Iraqi troops.

01:36:55.768 --> 01:36:58.437
Animal fats, and tallow
could no longer supply

01:36:58.437 --> 01:37:00.981
the ever growing need for
candles and lubricants.

01:37:01.732 --> 01:37:04.902
Whale oil, which had long
been the chief illuminant,

01:37:04.902 --> 01:37:06.570
was becoming scarce.

01:37:06.570 --> 01:37:08.989
Yet the demand for artificial
light was increasing.

01:37:08.989 --> 01:37:12.993
In 1850, a lamp capable
of efficiently burning

01:37:12.993 --> 01:37:15.788
a refined product of
petroleum was invented.

01:37:15.788 --> 01:37:16.580
- Oil!

01:37:16.580 --> 01:37:20.751
- Oil!

01:37:20.751 --> 01:37:22.670
- Early oil production,
they used steam power

01:37:22.670 --> 01:37:24.088
for the drilling,
for the riggings

01:37:24.088 --> 01:37:25.589
of different pieces
of equipment,

01:37:25.589 --> 01:37:27.299
and that steam power
probably was produced

01:37:27.299 --> 01:37:28.592
by burning wood or coal.

01:37:28.592 --> 01:37:30.594
So you'd use wood
or coal to get oil.

01:37:30.594 --> 01:37:32.263
Today, we use
electricity to get oil.

01:37:32.263 --> 01:37:33.514
We use gas to get oil.

01:37:33.514 --> 01:37:35.099
So we use one form of
energy to get another.

01:37:35.099 --> 01:37:37.434
And that's one of the
interconnected aspects

01:37:37.434 --> 01:37:39.270
of energy that's kinda
surprising for people

01:37:39.270 --> 01:37:40.771
is how interdependent is.

01:37:40.771 --> 01:37:43.274
We don't have an economy that
runs on one form of energy,

01:37:43.274 --> 01:37:45.985
we have an economy that
runs on many forms.

01:37:45.985 --> 01:37:49.446
(water splashing)

01:37:49.446 --> 01:37:51.824
(soft music)

01:37:59.665 --> 01:38:01.792
- Part of this equation,

01:38:01.792 --> 01:38:03.002
certainly in the short term

01:38:03.002 --> 01:38:05.170
to this time period
you're worried about,

01:38:05.170 --> 01:38:08.340
is modulating consumption and
adjusting cultural behavior.

01:38:08.340 --> 01:38:09.633
But people aren't
talking about that,

01:38:09.633 --> 01:38:11.135
no one wants to blame anybody.

01:38:11.135 --> 01:38:12.511
If you wanna blame industry,

01:38:12.511 --> 01:38:14.305
well, industry exists because
people buy their things

01:38:14.305 --> 01:38:15.472
or use their things.

01:38:15.472 --> 01:38:16.974
So they're the
ultimate consumers.

01:38:16.974 --> 01:38:19.059
Are we gonna carbon tax them?
Are we gonna punish them?

01:38:19.059 --> 01:38:20.561
Or maybe part of
the equation is,

01:38:20.561 --> 01:38:22.146
we all accept responsibility

01:38:22.146 --> 01:38:23.981
and we get a cultural
change to take place here

01:38:23.981 --> 01:38:26.191
to go back to where we were
20, 30 or 40 years ago.

01:38:26.191 --> 01:38:27.693
- We might, but that's hard.

01:38:27.693 --> 01:38:31.196
- The vast majority of our
individual impact comes from,

01:38:31.196 --> 01:38:34.158
housing, transport and food.

01:38:34.158 --> 01:38:36.201
These are kinda big
structural issues

01:38:36.201 --> 01:38:37.995
that can't really be
addressed adequately

01:38:37.995 --> 01:38:41.332
by changing the brand
of paper towels you buy.

01:38:41.332 --> 01:38:45.044
- We live in an energy
consumption society.

01:38:45.836 --> 01:38:49.590
And the way to look at things,

01:38:49.590 --> 01:38:54.428
we have to be aware that
nothing comes for free.

01:38:56.221 --> 01:38:58.724
We have to be aware that
everything costs energy,

01:38:58.724 --> 01:38:59.892
whatever you do.

01:39:01.060 --> 01:39:03.520
(soft music)

01:39:14.365 --> 01:39:17.368
- This is not what public
space looks like in the U.S,

01:39:17.368 --> 01:39:20.204
but I noticed here
in the Netherlands,

01:39:20.204 --> 01:39:22.414
everybody's riding
bikes everywhere.

01:39:22.414 --> 01:39:23.791
- Yeah. Yeah.

01:39:23.791 --> 01:39:28.420
- And that doesn't
strangely require gasoline.

01:39:29.546 --> 01:39:30.756
- Nope.

01:39:33.550 --> 01:39:36.136
(upbeat music)

01:39:39.390 --> 01:39:43.268
- The question
is, can we replace

01:39:43.268 --> 01:39:46.105
fossil fuels with
renewable energy?

01:39:46.105 --> 01:39:48.065
And this is a very
tricky question.

01:39:48.941 --> 01:39:53.654
It raises enormous debates

01:39:53.654 --> 01:39:58.492
and people get angry when
you asked them this question,

01:39:58.492 --> 01:40:01.787
because it is not
clear what you mean.

01:40:01.787 --> 01:40:06.583
If you mean that you can
satisfy people's greed

01:40:06.583 --> 01:40:08.669
with renewable energy,

01:40:08.669 --> 01:40:10.087
then the answer is no.

01:40:10.963 --> 01:40:14.466
If you mean renewable
energy could produce

01:40:15.300 --> 01:40:19.805
a sufficient amount of
energy in order to survive,

01:40:19.805 --> 01:40:21.682
then the answer is yes.

01:40:21.682 --> 01:40:25.936
- We are in an energy efficient
home now, that we've rebuilt

01:40:25.936 --> 01:40:32.443
that our household use
alone is twice the size

01:40:32.443 --> 01:40:34.778
of the average American,

01:40:34.778 --> 01:40:36.155
that woke me up,

01:40:36.155 --> 01:40:37.698
I can't count the
number of trips

01:40:37.698 --> 01:40:40.325
that I've taken around
the world for business,

01:40:40.325 --> 01:40:42.828
but that two
international trips,

01:40:44.288 --> 01:40:49.501
was equal to one year of
utility energy in my home.

01:40:49.501 --> 01:40:54.465
And that really had me
thinking about travel.

01:40:54.465 --> 01:40:57.801
It had me thinking
about what I eat.

01:41:00.304 --> 01:41:02.014
- [Michael] Another
surprise for many people

01:41:02.014 --> 01:41:04.183
is how much energy we
use in the food system.

01:41:04.183 --> 01:41:05.976
- [Narrator] And
complete dinners.

01:41:05.976 --> 01:41:07.352
- [Michael] In
the United States,

01:41:07.352 --> 01:41:09.229
we use 100 quadrillion
BTU of energy a year.

01:41:09.229 --> 01:41:11.315
A BTU is our British
thermal unit.

01:41:11.315 --> 01:41:13.859
One BTU is about the energy
content of a kitchen match.

01:41:13.859 --> 01:41:15.527
So in the United States, we use

01:41:15.527 --> 01:41:17.988
100 billion million kitchen
matches of energy per year.

01:41:22.826 --> 01:41:26.830
10 quads, 10 quadrillion
BTU is for the food system.

01:41:26.830 --> 01:41:28.373
One of those quads is
in the food itself.

01:41:28.373 --> 01:41:29.875
Food is a form of energy.

01:41:29.875 --> 01:41:32.377
We're ingesting at one quad
of food energy into our bodies

01:41:32.377 --> 01:41:34.588
for 330 million
Americans every year.

01:41:34.588 --> 01:41:37.257
The other nine quads is for
the rest of the food system.

01:41:37.257 --> 01:41:40.052
This is in the agrochemicals,
the fertilizers and pesticides

01:41:40.052 --> 01:41:43.180
diesel for tractors, the
energy for refrigeration,

01:41:43.180 --> 01:41:45.516
the energy for drying
crops at silos,

01:41:45.516 --> 01:41:46.767
warehouses, transportation,

01:41:46.767 --> 01:41:48.393
plus the energy to
cook at our homes.

01:41:48.393 --> 01:41:49.686
- [Narrator] Pies are done.

01:41:49.686 --> 01:41:50.896
- [Michael] Three or
four quads of that

01:41:50.896 --> 01:41:52.523
is just for the cold chain.

01:41:52.523 --> 01:41:53.857
That's the refrigeration system:

01:41:53.857 --> 01:41:56.276
refrigerated trucks,
refrigerated warehouses,

01:41:56.276 --> 01:41:59.363
and refrigerators and freezers
at our homes and businesses.

01:41:59.363 --> 01:42:01.406
The energy embedded
in the cold chain

01:42:01.406 --> 01:42:03.075
of the food system
in the United States

01:42:03.075 --> 01:42:04.701
is more energy than
entire countries

01:42:04.701 --> 01:42:07.579
like Switzerland and Sweden
consume in a year combined.

01:42:12.251 --> 01:42:15.129
The energy embedded in the
edible food we throw away

01:42:15.129 --> 01:42:18.632
is also enough to power
entire countries elsewhere.

01:42:18.632 --> 01:42:20.050
Sadly, we throw away like

01:42:20.050 --> 01:42:21.927
a fourth to a half
of our edible food.

01:42:23.095 --> 01:42:25.722
(upbeat music)

01:42:29.768 --> 01:42:31.645
- [Wesley] Okay so
Michael, here we are.

01:42:31.645 --> 01:42:32.604
We're surrounded by water.

01:42:32.604 --> 01:42:33.814
- [Michael] Yeah.

01:42:33.814 --> 01:42:34.773
- [Wesley] And what's
the relationship

01:42:34.773 --> 01:42:36.275
between water and energy?

01:42:36.275 --> 01:42:38.110
- [Michael] The water energy
relationship is twofold.

01:42:38.110 --> 01:42:40.279
One is we use a lot
of water for energy.

01:42:40.279 --> 01:42:41.947
We use water to
cool power plants,

01:42:41.947 --> 01:42:44.324
we use water to
irrigate biofuels,

01:42:44.324 --> 01:42:45.784
we use water for oil
and gas production,

01:42:45.784 --> 01:42:47.578
we use water to make
steam at refineries.

01:42:47.578 --> 01:42:49.454
In fact, if you look all up and
down the energy supply chain

01:42:49.454 --> 01:42:51.165
water's at every step.

01:42:51.165 --> 01:42:53.000
- Our well went dry.

01:42:53.000 --> 01:42:53.834
- Dead.

01:42:53.834 --> 01:42:55.335
- Yeah, that's dead.

01:42:55.335 --> 01:42:58.172
- This is where the water,
that would be the full mark.

01:42:58.172 --> 01:43:01.925
It's not a fantasy
apocalypse movie landscape.

01:43:01.925 --> 01:43:04.428
It's now and it's real.

01:43:04.428 --> 01:43:05.762
- So the grid might be strained

01:43:05.762 --> 01:43:07.598
if you don't have water
available for cooling,

01:43:07.598 --> 01:43:09.516
at least the way we have
most power plants today,

01:43:09.516 --> 01:43:10.767
where they use heat,

01:43:10.767 --> 01:43:12.811
they then need a
water for cooling.

01:43:12.811 --> 01:43:15.105
Modern forms of like solar
panels or wind turbines

01:43:15.105 --> 01:43:16.815
don't need water coolings.

01:43:16.815 --> 01:43:20.110
- The average life for PV
panels is what, 20 years?

01:43:20.110 --> 01:43:20.986
- 20, 25 years.

01:43:20.986 --> 01:43:22.279
- 20, 25 years.

01:43:22.279 --> 01:43:24.531
- Maybe 30 years, yeah.
- So the people who say,

01:43:24.531 --> 01:43:26.450
well, it takes seven
years of energy

01:43:26.450 --> 01:43:29.203
to produce this PV panel,
but then you're getting,

01:43:29.203 --> 01:43:31.955
that means you're getting 18
years of energy free.

01:43:31.955 --> 01:43:33.457
So you've cut down.

01:43:33.457 --> 01:43:36.168
- Even then you're getting 18
years of energy for free.

01:43:37.294 --> 01:43:38.503
- So me John Q Public,

01:43:38.503 --> 01:43:39.796
I'm like, hey man,

01:43:39.796 --> 01:43:41.632
I wanna put some money
into your solar farm.

01:43:41.632 --> 01:43:45.844
- No, not until the government
gets involved with things,

01:43:45.844 --> 01:43:49.806
puts us into a pari passu
position with fossil fuels.

01:43:49.806 --> 01:43:51.141
- I've got a lot of friends

01:43:51.141 --> 01:43:52.517
who are in the
renewable business,

01:43:52.517 --> 01:43:55.062
and there's a really
big problem in terms of,

01:43:55.062 --> 01:43:56.855
you know, you've got the
power purchase agreement,

01:43:56.855 --> 01:43:58.690
you've got all the
permitting done,

01:43:58.690 --> 01:44:00.817
You've even got
interconnect agreements,

01:44:00.817 --> 01:44:02.361
except what's happening is,

01:44:02.361 --> 01:44:04.363
grid companies are delaying

01:44:04.363 --> 01:44:05.572
and not letting
them on the grid.

01:44:05.572 --> 01:44:06.823
What's going on with that?

01:44:06.823 --> 01:44:09.034
- I think one of the
challenges we have is,

01:44:09.034 --> 01:44:10.702
who has access to
the infrastructure

01:44:10.702 --> 01:44:12.704
and democratizing
the infrastructure

01:44:12.704 --> 01:44:14.331
is a way to open it up so that

01:44:14.331 --> 01:44:16.875
more solutions can
be brought forward.

01:44:16.875 --> 01:44:18.085
This is something that we did

01:44:18.085 --> 01:44:20.379
with telecommunications
in the 80s with AT&amp;T,

01:44:20.379 --> 01:44:22.214
AT&amp;T had built out all
the wires and poles,

01:44:22.214 --> 01:44:23.715
and they were
required to open up

01:44:23.715 --> 01:44:25.217
their infrastructure
for other companies.

01:44:25.217 --> 01:44:27.344
And that's why we have
so many phone companies,

01:44:27.344 --> 01:44:30.055
and long distance companies
now that we didn't have before.

01:44:30.055 --> 01:44:31.556
The same thing could happen

01:44:31.556 --> 01:44:33.684
with democratizing the
electrical infrastructure.

01:44:33.684 --> 01:44:35.727
More solutions would be
plugged in more wind and solar,

01:44:35.727 --> 01:44:37.062
and we have more
technologies along the way.

01:44:37.062 --> 01:44:39.106
And this is where
long range planning,

01:44:39.106 --> 01:44:41.066
stable governance really matter.

01:44:41.066 --> 01:44:42.567
- Kind of what I'm hearing is,

01:44:42.567 --> 01:44:44.903
it's not a technology problem.

01:44:44.903 --> 01:44:46.196
- No, not a technology problem.

01:44:46.196 --> 01:44:47.281
- It's not an
engineering problem.

01:44:47.281 --> 01:44:49.700
- No.
- It's a financial
engineering problem.

01:44:53.203 --> 01:44:58.125
- [Luka] Energy poverty, lack
of food, income inequality.

01:44:58.125 --> 01:45:01.128
So it's up to us now
to form the policy

01:45:01.128 --> 01:45:05.132
that is going to
help bridge this gap.

01:45:05.132 --> 01:45:08.593
- In 1941, when the
United States went to war,

01:45:08.593 --> 01:45:11.305
and people saved tin
cans and aluminum

01:45:11.305 --> 01:45:15.892
and gave up, women gave
up buying nylon stockings,

01:45:15.892 --> 01:45:18.729
and everything was done
for the war effort.

01:45:18.729 --> 01:45:21.398
But the war was there,
was right in front of us.

01:45:21.398 --> 01:45:24.276
And young men were being
drafted and sent overseas,

01:45:24.276 --> 01:45:25.944
and casualties were coming home.

01:45:25.944 --> 01:45:28.113
And this is different.

01:45:28.113 --> 01:45:33.118
It requires the same type of
reorganization or commitment.

01:45:34.077 --> 01:45:34.953
- But why didn't
they call on me?

01:45:34.953 --> 01:45:35.912
I could have done something.

01:45:35.912 --> 01:45:38.248
(jazz music)

01:45:47.174 --> 01:45:49.134
- That was then,
and this is now.

01:45:49.134 --> 01:45:50.635
Check out our new lot

01:45:50.635 --> 01:45:54.139
the okay solutions lot with
millions of new solutions

01:45:54.139 --> 01:45:56.350
and more coming
every single day.

01:45:56.350 --> 01:45:59.102
Looking for something that's
easy and in your price range,

01:45:59.102 --> 01:46:00.854
check out our
pre-owned solutions.

01:46:00.854 --> 01:46:03.482
Like changing a light
bulb or walking to work,

01:46:03.482 --> 01:46:06.651
or our mid-sized selections
like regenerative agriculture,

01:46:06.651 --> 01:46:09.321
or a price on carbon,
or you'll love a variety

01:46:09.321 --> 01:46:12.991
of brand new solutions with
that brand new solution smell.

01:46:12.991 --> 01:46:15.660
Like de-leveraging climate risk
from the derivatives market,

01:46:15.660 --> 01:46:18.955
or the million mile battery,
which is coming soon.

01:46:18.955 --> 01:46:22.000
Just listen to one of
our satisfied customers.

01:46:22.000 --> 01:46:24.127
- Michael, you are now in
charge of the United States

01:46:24.127 --> 01:46:27.506
Energy policy and
you have free reign.

01:46:27.506 --> 01:46:29.216
How would we make
the transition?

01:46:29.216 --> 01:46:31.468
- For national security reasons
and for economic reasons

01:46:31.468 --> 01:46:32.636
and for environmental reasons

01:46:32.636 --> 01:46:34.012
there are many
reasons to do this.

01:46:34.012 --> 01:46:37.808
We need to harden, improve
and expand our grid.

01:46:37.808 --> 01:46:39.684
Maybe even using the
interstate highways.

01:46:39.684 --> 01:46:41.353
We already have the rights
of way and put lines

01:46:41.353 --> 01:46:44.147
above ground or below ground,
create a national network.

01:46:44.147 --> 01:46:47.317
And then it will span and
reach to the sunny windy areas,

01:46:47.317 --> 01:46:49.319
and already connects
to the urban areas.

01:46:51.363 --> 01:46:52.531
And once we have the network,

01:46:52.531 --> 01:46:54.157
we can plug in whatever we want,

01:46:54.157 --> 01:46:56.701
build the infrastructure first,
then the energy will follow.

01:46:56.701 --> 01:46:58.203
But we have to put a
price on pollution.

01:46:58.203 --> 01:47:00.372
We'll take the money from
the price on pollution

01:47:00.372 --> 01:47:02.040
to clean up the trash.

01:47:02.040 --> 01:47:02.999
So let's take the United States

01:47:02.999 --> 01:47:04.709
6 billion tons of CO2 a year,

01:47:04.709 --> 01:47:05.919
put in the atmosphere.

01:47:05.919 --> 01:47:08.213
And let's say we're
willing to pay

01:47:08.213 --> 01:47:11.341
I don't know, $30
a ton to remove it.

01:47:11.341 --> 01:47:13.051
That works out to if I
do my math correctly,

01:47:13.051 --> 01:47:14.719
$180 billion a year.

01:47:14.719 --> 01:47:16.054
Sounds like a lot of money.

01:47:16.054 --> 01:47:17.347
That's less than what we spend

01:47:17.347 --> 01:47:19.057
on just dealing with
trash from cities.

01:47:20.350 --> 01:47:22.394
Why not spend another couple
hundred billion cleaning

01:47:22.394 --> 01:47:24.020
up the waste in the atmosphere.

01:47:24.855 --> 01:47:26.731
Let's take the CO2
and put it into the soil.

01:47:26.731 --> 01:47:28.525
Like, let's pay farmers
to take the Co2

01:47:28.525 --> 01:47:30.402
out of the atmosphere
into the soil.

01:47:30.402 --> 01:47:33.071
(soft gentle music)

01:47:38.910 --> 01:47:41.746
We need to get more of that
national security mindset.

01:47:45.584 --> 01:47:47.085
- If you have a built
to last strategy,

01:47:47.085 --> 01:47:49.254
then you do want to look at

01:47:49.254 --> 01:47:51.381
what's happening in
these longer horizons

01:47:51.381 --> 01:47:53.425
and it's absolutely critical.

01:47:53.425 --> 01:47:56.386
- It's gonna take
all forms of energy

01:47:56.386 --> 01:47:59.931
and all people to move us
through this transition.

01:47:59.931 --> 01:48:03.226
- Otherwise, I don't think
we actually stand a chance.

01:48:03.226 --> 01:48:05.604
(jazz music)

01:48:06.563 --> 01:48:08.940
- I'm trying to get
rid of this car,

01:48:08.940 --> 01:48:12.736
but it's like quitting
smoking, it's not easy.

01:48:12.736 --> 01:48:15.447
You are so used
to certain things

01:48:15.447 --> 01:48:17.782
that it is very difficult.

01:48:20.076 --> 01:48:23.330
- The Romans had done
a lot of bad things

01:48:23.330 --> 01:48:24.581
to the ecosystem.

01:48:24.581 --> 01:48:25.957
- Yeah.

01:48:25.957 --> 01:48:30.253
- But, they had not
destroyed it as we could do.

01:48:30.253 --> 01:48:35.258
- FEMA estimates for
every dollar spent
on hazard mitigation

01:48:35.258 --> 01:48:38.762
you save $4 in disaster
recovery costs.

01:48:40.180 --> 01:48:41.932
So we're already
spending the money.

01:48:41.932 --> 01:48:44.184
There's the sense that
it's perhaps too expensive

01:48:44.184 --> 01:48:45.852
to prepare for climate change,

01:48:45.852 --> 01:48:47.646
but we are already
spending the money.

01:48:47.646 --> 01:48:50.482
We're spending it after
every disaster hits.

01:48:50.482 --> 01:48:52.609
(airplane humming)

01:48:52.609 --> 01:48:54.986
(jazz music)

01:48:57.822 --> 01:49:00.825
- When I was growing
up, there was the draft.

01:49:00.825 --> 01:49:03.286
So everybody thought they had
an obligation to the country.

01:49:03.286 --> 01:49:07.040
If you went to a land-grant
college you were drafted.

01:49:07.040 --> 01:49:09.501
Now it's different.

01:49:09.501 --> 01:49:11.962
The trust in government's gone,

01:49:11.962 --> 01:49:14.339
the obligation to
government is gone.

01:49:14.339 --> 01:49:17.801
If we could take some of
that spirit of patriotism

01:49:17.801 --> 01:49:22.639
and service and rebuild
it into doing something

01:49:22.639 --> 01:49:23.974
for the environment.

01:49:25.183 --> 01:49:27.644
- This entire thing
is built out of carbon

01:49:27.644 --> 01:49:29.312
that comes out of
the atmosphere.

01:49:32.357 --> 01:49:34.234
- We need a hundred
thousand young people

01:49:34.234 --> 01:49:36.528
to give up part of their summer

01:49:36.528 --> 01:49:39.239
and form up and
really work this.

01:49:39.239 --> 01:49:41.324
We could control
these pine beetles

01:49:41.324 --> 01:49:42.826
that are destroying the forest.

01:49:42.826 --> 01:49:45.829
We could plant new trees, but
it should be done not only

01:49:45.829 --> 01:49:50.709
for pay as a guaranteed job,
but it should also be done

01:49:50.709 --> 01:49:54.879
for every citizen's obligation
to take care of the land.

01:49:54.879 --> 01:49:56.381
That's what I'd like to see.

01:49:56.381 --> 01:49:57.549
- It sounds like you're trying
to inspire a whole generation

01:49:57.549 --> 01:49:58.717
of people to pay for it.

01:49:58.717 --> 01:50:00.719
- Well, it sounds like something
that old guys would say.

01:50:00.719 --> 01:50:02.053
(laughs)

01:50:02.053 --> 01:50:03.013
- Whoa.

01:50:03.013 --> 01:50:04.889
- Baby baby.

01:50:04.889 --> 01:50:06.349
Can you smile for us?

01:50:07.892 --> 01:50:09.227
That's a good noise.

01:50:10.061 --> 01:50:12.731
It's made me want to be more
of a fighter for her sake.

01:50:13.231 --> 01:50:16.568
The transformation is
potentially exciting,

01:50:16.568 --> 01:50:18.236
because we can think not only

01:50:18.236 --> 01:50:20.947
about losing things that
we have always wanted

01:50:20.947 --> 01:50:22.282
but also letting go of things

01:50:22.282 --> 01:50:24.743
that we perhaps
don't benefit from.

01:50:25.535 --> 01:50:28.913
We can lose in order to
transform into something else.

01:50:28.913 --> 01:50:32.584
It's not as kind of confident
that things will be okay,

01:50:32.584 --> 01:50:34.919
but it finds possibility
in a ways that

01:50:34.919 --> 01:50:36.046
things will have to change

01:50:36.046 --> 01:50:38.757
in kind of really dramatic ways.

01:50:39.215 --> 01:50:43.136
(speaking in foreign language)

01:51:02.572 --> 01:51:05.408
- And so we are looking
at diversifying the types

01:51:05.408 --> 01:51:08.286
of crops that we are
wanting to plant,

01:51:08.286 --> 01:51:12.123
and also work with
conservation type programs

01:51:12.123 --> 01:51:14.918
to help preserve
species and habitat.

01:51:14.918 --> 01:51:17.462
And let nature take it's course

01:51:17.462 --> 01:51:20.965
and bring back
species that we need

01:51:20.965 --> 01:51:24.636
and that the crops actually
need to be productive.

01:51:24.636 --> 01:51:27.013
(jazz music)

01:51:30.475 --> 01:51:33.269
- [Warren] We don't want a
hurricane or a tropical storm,

01:51:33.269 --> 01:51:35.814
they're already geared up
the crops are turned in.

01:51:35.814 --> 01:51:37.649
They wanna go ahead
and get finished.

01:51:37.649 --> 01:51:39.317
People wanna know where
their food came from.

01:51:39.317 --> 01:51:41.528
Well, at some point maybe
people will wanna know

01:51:41.528 --> 01:51:43.113
where their banker
lives and who he is.

01:51:43.113 --> 01:51:44.823
And do I have a
relationship with him

01:51:44.823 --> 01:51:47.867
that when I need some
money to fix something

01:51:47.867 --> 01:51:49.953
I can maybe find
somebody to help me?

01:51:51.496 --> 01:51:53.123
- Yeah, I mean,
it's not a matter of

01:51:53.123 --> 01:51:54.958
the government
telling banks what to do,

01:51:54.958 --> 01:51:56.835
or telling us what we can do.

01:51:56.835 --> 01:51:59.796
No, I mean, there's still
lots of possibilities.

01:51:59.796 --> 01:52:03.675
We should have smaller banks
basically split them up,

01:52:03.675 --> 01:52:06.344
and not make them
too big to fail.

01:52:06.344 --> 01:52:09.848
If they make mistakes, they
should fail without causing

01:52:09.848 --> 01:52:11.683
a total systems crisis.

01:52:13.226 --> 01:52:16.646
And probably what also needs
to be done is introduce rules

01:52:16.646 --> 01:52:19.524
which make it impossible
for the financial sector

01:52:19.524 --> 01:52:22.819
to create within the
financial sector itself

01:52:22.819 --> 01:52:24.654
so much additional debt.

01:52:27.198 --> 01:52:31.828
- We have to somehow correct
this market imperfection.

01:52:31.828 --> 01:52:34.038
- If you want to
have a derivative,

01:52:34.038 --> 01:52:36.082
if you want to hedge
your risk fine,

01:52:36.082 --> 01:52:38.543
but you must have exposure
to the underlying asset.

01:52:40.712 --> 01:52:42.922
- [Robert] I remember
I was watching on TV

01:52:42.922 --> 01:52:45.341
after a major flood
down in Florida,

01:52:45.341 --> 01:52:47.552
and they were interviewing
this guy and he said,

01:52:47.552 --> 01:52:51.514
"My father rebuilt twice, my
grandfather rebuilt twice."

01:52:51.514 --> 01:52:53.725
And he says, "And I'm
gonna rebuild again".

01:52:53.725 --> 01:52:55.435
And the reporter turned
into the camera and said,

01:52:55.435 --> 01:52:57.103
"That's the American way".

01:52:57.103 --> 01:53:01.232
Well, I thought the reporter
should say, that's stupid.

01:53:03.401 --> 01:53:06.279
You can't keep doing
it over and over again

01:53:06.279 --> 01:53:08.281
that's the definition
of insanity.

01:53:10.742 --> 01:53:15.079
- So it's basically trust
in the monetary management

01:53:15.079 --> 01:53:18.208
and trust in the future
productivity of a country

01:53:18.208 --> 01:53:20.710
that gives us trust
in the currency.

01:53:20.710 --> 01:53:23.421
Not the idea that there
are some gold barons hidden

01:53:23.421 --> 01:53:24.547
in the federal reserve.

01:53:24.547 --> 01:53:26.925
(jazz music)

01:53:28.218 --> 01:53:31.596
- And so there is a
point of resistance

01:53:31.596 --> 01:53:34.724
at which governments
aren't going to be,

01:53:35.391 --> 01:53:38.937
have access to capital
to address the problem.

01:53:40.396 --> 01:53:42.106
- We're rich.

01:53:42.106 --> 01:53:43.900
(thunderstorm rumbling)

01:53:43.900 --> 01:53:45.235
- You know, the fear is,

01:53:45.235 --> 01:53:46.945
if we cannot bail out
the system anymore

01:53:46.945 --> 01:53:49.781
it will come crushing down
with huge side effects.

01:53:50.740 --> 01:53:52.659
The hope and that
sounds a little cynical

01:53:52.659 --> 01:53:56.079
but I think the hope is
we probably can reorder

01:53:56.079 --> 01:53:58.581
our system only after a crash.

01:53:58.581 --> 01:54:00.750
And I think that human
history is such that

01:54:00.750 --> 01:54:03.670
we are unable to make
really fundamental change

01:54:03.670 --> 01:54:06.589
in the way we live and the
way we organize ourselves

01:54:06.589 --> 01:54:08.132
without such a crash.

01:54:08.132 --> 01:54:11.261
Nobody wants it, and I'm the
last person who wants it.

01:54:11.261 --> 01:54:14.639
And if I think back to the
1930s, and I'm German, right?

01:54:14.639 --> 01:54:16.474
And you think about
what the repercussions

01:54:16.474 --> 01:54:19.018
of a major crisis can be.

01:54:19.018 --> 01:54:20.937
We just don't know how
we would get out on

01:54:20.937 --> 01:54:22.605
the other end of the tunnel.

01:54:22.605 --> 01:54:25.108
I think without this
crisis, I just don't see

01:54:25.108 --> 01:54:27.819
enough political leverage
to fundamentally alter

01:54:27.819 --> 01:54:30.446
the way we organize
our financial system.

01:54:31.656 --> 01:54:33.366
- When climate change happens,

01:54:34.325 --> 01:54:37.120
there will be real problems
and it will be real,

01:54:37.120 --> 01:54:39.038
as you said, there will
be real scarcities,

01:54:39.038 --> 01:54:41.666
and it will be real migrants
coming, and there will be an...

01:54:41.666 --> 01:54:43.209
- And that's what I,

01:54:43.209 --> 01:54:45.169
and then will really be
broke because it'll be

01:54:45.169 --> 01:54:48.464
after you've had that collapse
of the inverted pyramid.

01:54:48.464 --> 01:54:49.716
And then it's like, then it's

01:54:49.716 --> 01:54:52.385
like asking Somalia to
fight climate change.

01:54:52.385 --> 01:54:53.344
- Yeah, yeah.

01:54:55.847 --> 01:54:58.349
- [Wesley] So your
criticism would be

01:54:58.349 --> 01:55:00.018
that we're not facing reality

01:55:00.018 --> 01:55:01.853
and not adapting to it?

01:55:01.853 --> 01:55:03.313
- That's correct.

01:55:03.313 --> 01:55:05.690
- My answer to that is, I
still have faith in democracy.

01:55:05.690 --> 01:55:07.692
I believe we can
do it as a people.

01:55:07.692 --> 01:55:10.028
I believe we can
do it as a society.

01:55:10.028 --> 01:55:11.821
And I believe we can
take the institutions

01:55:11.821 --> 01:55:14.908
of the United States that have
served this country very well

01:55:14.908 --> 01:55:18.036
for over 200 years, and
move into the 21st century

01:55:18.036 --> 01:55:20.496
and still help lead
mankind forward.

01:55:21.205 --> 01:55:24.167
It's an article of
faith, but it's the faith

01:55:24.167 --> 01:55:25.501
that motivates action.

01:55:26.336 --> 01:55:28.338
We have almost all the tools

01:55:28.338 --> 01:55:30.506
and all the financial resources,

01:55:30.506 --> 01:55:34.385
and all the institutions that
could solve this problem.

01:55:34.385 --> 01:55:38.348
It's the kind of challenge that
could bring mankind together

01:55:38.348 --> 01:55:40.850
in a way we've never been
brought together before.

01:55:42.018 --> 01:55:44.228
- I wish there was
more conversation,

01:55:44.228 --> 01:55:47.899
more guys on the opposite
side talking together.

01:55:47.899 --> 01:55:49.776
Because everybody as we feel,

01:55:49.776 --> 01:55:51.736
there are people feeling
opposite from us

01:55:51.736 --> 01:55:54.280
that are just as righteous
and all of that stuff.

01:55:54.280 --> 01:55:56.741
But we ought to get
together to see...

01:55:56.741 --> 01:55:59.077
- The thing if you get
together with people like this,

01:55:59.077 --> 01:56:01.245
what you find is
the disagreements

01:56:01.245 --> 01:56:04.082
are pretty narrow actually.
- Yes, that's what I mean.

01:56:04.082 --> 01:56:07.418
- The people are mostly
agreed on like 95%.

01:56:07.418 --> 01:56:08.628
- Yeah.

01:56:08.628 --> 01:56:10.880
- But, but there are
forces in the culture

01:56:10.880 --> 01:56:14.926
that want to sharpen and
popularize those disagreement.

01:56:14.926 --> 01:56:15.885
- Yeah.

01:56:15.885 --> 01:56:17.220
- It's a human nature issue.

01:56:17.220 --> 01:56:18.054
- Okay?

01:56:18.054 --> 01:56:18.972
- So what sells?

01:56:20.390 --> 01:56:21.265
Tell me, what sells?

01:56:21.265 --> 01:56:22.433
What do you pay attention to

01:56:22.433 --> 01:56:23.434
when it's happening around you?

01:56:23.434 --> 01:56:24.394
- You gotta find people--

01:56:24.394 --> 01:56:25.895
- Conflict, conflict, conflict.

01:56:25.895 --> 01:56:27.105
- It's true. It's true.
- That's it.

01:56:27.105 --> 01:56:30.274
- To create that
kind of a dialogue

01:56:30.274 --> 01:56:31.776
between a father and a son,

01:56:31.776 --> 01:56:34.487
that love thing, I
mean, this is my kid.

01:56:34.487 --> 01:56:37.073
You guys must have some
wild conversation man.

01:56:37.073 --> 01:56:38.616
- It's what gets me.

01:56:38.616 --> 01:56:40.785
I totally...
- This is the basis

01:56:40.785 --> 01:56:42.578
- disagree with that.
- of democracy and capitalism.

01:56:42.578 --> 01:56:44.622
- No, I disagree with that.

01:56:44.622 --> 01:56:47.250
(wind blowing)

01:56:56.801 --> 01:56:59.804
(gentle jazz music)

